\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* He who (the heart of) all matters hath proven let him ⌜teach⌝ the nation,
\q1 ⌜He who all⌝ knowledge possesseth, therein shall he ⌜school⌝ all the people,
\q1 ⌜He shall his wisdom impart (?)⌝ and (so) shall they ⌝share it⌝ together.
\q1 ⌜Gilgamish(?)⌝—he was the ⌜Master⌝ of wisdom, with ⌜knowledge⌝ of all things,
\v 2 \vp 1.5\vp* He ’twas discovered the secret concealed . . . . . . . . .
\q1 (Aye), handed down the tradition relating to (things) prediluvian,
\q1 Went on a journey afar, (all) aweary and ⌜worn with his toiling(?)⌝,
\v 3 \vp 1.10\vp* ⌜Graved⌝ on a table of stone all the travail.
\q1 Of Erech, the high-wall’d,
\q1 He (it was) built up the ramparts; (and) he (it was) clamp’d the foundation,
\q1 Like unto brass, of ⌜E⌝-Anna, the sacred, the treasury hallow’d,
\q1 ⌜Strengthen’d⌝ its base to grant wayleave to no ⌜one⌝ . . . . . .
\q1 . . . . . the threshold which from ⌜of old (?)⌝ . . . . . .
\q1 . . . . . ⌜E⌝-Anna . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
\v 4 \vp 1.15\vp* . . . . . to grant wayleave ⌜to no one (?)⌝ . . . . . . .
\v 5 \vp 2.1\vp* Two-thirds of him are divine, and ⌜one-third of him human,⌝ . . .
\q1 The form of his body . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
\q1 He hath forced to take . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
\v 6 \vp 2.7\vp* “. . . . . . . . of Erech ’tis he who hath ⌜taken⌝,
\q1 . . . . . . . . . (while) tow’reth ⌜his⌝ crest like an aurochs,
\v 7 \vp 2.10\vp* Ne’er hath the shock of ⌜his⌝ weapons (its) ⌜peer⌝; are driven ⌜his⌝ fellows
\q1 Into the toils, while cow’d are the heroes of Erech un- . . . . .
\q1 Gilgamish leaveth no son to ⌜his⌝ father, ⌜his⌝ arrogance swelling
\q1 (Each) day and ⌜night⌝; ⌜aye, he⌝ is the shepherd of Erech, the high-⌜wall’d⌝,
\v 8 \vp 2.15\vp* He is ⌜our(?)⌝ shepherd . . . . ⌜masterful, dominant, subtle⌝ . . .
\q1 ⌜Gilgamish⌝ leaveth no ⌜maid to her mother, nor⌝ daughter to ⌜hero⌝,
\q1 ⌜(Nay), nor a spouse to a husband⌝”
\q1 (And so), to (th’ appeal of) their wailing
\q1 ⌜Gave ear th’ Immortals⌝: the gods of high heaven ⌜address’d the god Anu⌝,
\v 9 \vp 2.20\vp* (Him who was) Seigneur of Erech: “’Tis thou a son hast begotten,
\q1 (Aye, in sooth, all) tyrannous, ⌜while tow’reth his crest like an aurochs⌝,
\q1 Ne’er hath ⌜the shock of his weapons⌝ (its) peer; are driven ⌜his fellows⌝
\q1 Into the toils, ⌜while cow’d are the heroes of Erech un- . . . . ⌝.
\q1 Gilgamish leaveth no son to his father, ⌜his arrogance swelling⌝
\q1 (Each) day and night; aye, he is the shepherd of Erech, ⌜the high-wall’d⌝,
\v 10 \vp 2.25\vp* He is their shepherd . . . masterful, dominant, subtle . . .
\q1 Gilgamish leaveth no maid to ⌜her mother⌝, nor daughter to hero,
\q1 (Nay), nor a spouse to a ⌜husband⌝.”
\q1 (And so), to (th’ appeal of) their wailing
\v 11 \vp 2.30\vp* ⌜Anu⌝ gave ear, call’d the lady Aruru: “’Twas thou, O Aruru,
\q1 Madest ⌜(primeval seed of) mankind(?)⌝: do now make its fellow,
\q1 So that he ⌜happen on Gilgamish⌝, yea, on the day of his pleasure,
\q1 So that they strive with each other, and he unto Erech give ⌜surcease⌝.”
\v 12 \vp 2.34\vp* So when the goddess Aruru heard this, in her mind she imagined
\q1 (Straightway, this) Concept of Anu, and, washing her hands, (then) Aruru
\q1 Finger’d some clay, on the desert she moulded (it): ⌜(thus) on the desert⌝
\v 13 \vp 2.35\vp* Enkidu made she, a warrior, (as he were) born (and) begotten,
\q1 (Yea), of Ninurta the double, ⌜and put forth⌝ the whole of his body
\q1 Hair: in the way of a woman he snooded his locks (in a fillet);
\q1 Sprouted luxuriant growth of his hair-like (the awns of) the barley,
\q1 Nor knew he people nor land; he was clad in a garb like Sumuqan.
\v 14 \vp 2.40\vp* E’en with gazelles did he pasture on herbage, along with the cattle
\q1 Drank he his fill, with the beasts did his heart delight at the water.
\v 15 \vp 2.42\vp* (Then) did a hunter, a trapper, come face to face with this (fellow),
\q1 Came on him ⌜one⌝, two, three days, at the place where (the beasts) drank (their) water;
\v 16 \vp 2.45\vp* (Sooth), when the hunter espied him, his face o’ermantled with terror,
\q1 He and his cattle went unto his steading, ⌜dismay’d⌝ (and) affrighted,
\q1 Crying aloud, ⌜distress’d in⌝ his heart, (and) his face overclouded,
\q1 . . . . woe in his belly . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
\v 17 \vp 2.50\vp* (Aye, and) his face was the same as of one ⌜who hath gone⌝ a far ⌜journey⌝.
\v 18 \vp 3.1\vp* Open’d ⌜his mouth (then)⌝ the hunter, and spake, addressing ⌜his father⌝:
\q1 “Father, there is ⌜a⌝ great fellow come ⌜forth from out of the mountains⌝,
\q1 (O, but) ⌜his⌝ strength is the greatest ⌜(the length and breadth) of the country⌝,
\q1 ⌜Like to a double⌝ of Anu’s own self ⌜his strength⌝ is enormous,
\v 19 \vp 3.5\vp* Ever (?) ⌜he rangeth at large⌝ o’er the mountains, ⌜(and) ever⌝ with cattle
\q1 ⌜Grazeth on herbage (and) ever he setteth⌝ his foot to the water,
\q1 ⌜So that I fear⌝ to approach him. The pits which I ⌜myself⌝ hollow’d
\v 20 \vp 3.10\vp* ⌜(With mine own hands) hath he fill’d in (again)⌝, (and) the traps of my ⌜setting⌝
\q1 ⌜Torn up, (and) out of my clutches hath holpen escape⌝ (all) the cattle,
\q1 Beasts of the desert: to work at my fieldcraft ⌜he will not allow⌝ me.”
\v 21 \vp 3.13\vp* ⌜Open’d his mouth (then) his father, and spake⌝, addressing the hunter:
\v 22 \vp 3.15\vp* “Gilgamish ⌜dwelleth⌝ in Erech, ⌜my son, whom no one⌝ hath vanquish’d,
\q1 ⌜(Nay, but) ’tis his strength is greatest (the length and breadth) of the country⌝
\q1 ⌜Like to a double of Anu’s own self⌝, his strength is ⌜enormous⌝,
\q1 ⌜Go, set⌝ thy face ⌜towards Erech: and when he hears of⌝ a monster,
\q1 ⌜He will say ‘Go, O hunter, a courtesan-girl, a hetaera⌝
\v 23 \vp 3.20\vp* Take ⌜with thee⌝ . . . . . . . like a strong one;
\q1 ⌜When he the cattle shall gather again⌝ to the place of (their) drinking,
\q1 ⌜So shall she put off⌝ her ⌜mantle⌝ (the charm of) her beauty ⌜revealing⌝;
\q1 ⌜(Then) shall he spy her, and (sooth) will embrace her, (and thenceforth) his cattle,
\q1 ⌜Which in⌝ his very own deserts ⌜were rear’d⌝, will (straightway) deny him.’”
\v 24 \vp 3.25\vp* Unto the rede of his father the hunter ⌜hath hearken’d, (and straightway)⌝
\q1 He will away ⌜unto Gilgamish⌝.
\q1 Taking the road towards Erech
\q1 Turn’d he ⌜his steps, and to⌝ Gilgamish ⌜came, his speech thus addressing⌝:
\q1 (Saying): “There is a great fellow ⌜come forth from out of the mountains⌝,
\v 25 \vp 3.30\vp* ⌜(O, but) his strength⌝ is the greatest, (the length and breadth) of the country,
\q1 Like to a double of Anu’s own self ⌜his strength⌝ is enormous,
\q1 ⌜Ever (?)⌝ he rangeth at large o’er the mountains, (and) ever with cattle
\q1 ⌜Grazeth on herbage, (and)⌝ ever ⌜he setteth⌝ his foot to the water,
\v 26 \vp 3.35\vp* So that I fear to approach ⌜him⌝ . The pits which I ⌜myself⌝ hollow’d
\q1 (With mine own hands) hath he fill’d in (again, and) the traps of my ⌜setting⌝
\q1 Torn up, (and) out of my clutches hath holpen escape (all) the cattle,
\q1 Beasts ⌜of the desert⌝: to work at my fieldcraft he will not allow me.”
\v 27 \vp 3.40\vp* Gilgamish unto him, unto the hunter made answer (in this wise):
\q1 “Go, (good) my hunter, take with thee a courtesan-girl, a hetaera,
\q1 When he the cattle shall ⌜gather⌝ again to the place of (their) drinking,
\q1 So shall she put off her mantle, (the charm of her) beauty ⌜revealing⌝,
\v 28 \vp 3.45\vp* (Then) shall he spy her, and (sooth) will embrace her, (and thenceforth) his cattle
\q1 Which in his very own deserts were rear’d will (straightway) deny him.”
\v 29 \vp 3.47\vp* Forth went the hunter, took with him a courtesan-girl, a hetaera,
\q1 (So) did they start on their travels, went forth on their journey (together),
\q1 (Aye), at the term of three days arrived at the pleasaunce appointed.
\q1 Sate they down in their ambush (?), the hunter and the hetaera,
\v 30 \vp 3.50\vp* One day, two days they sat by the place where (the beasts) drank (their) water.
\q1 (Then) at last came the cattle to take their fill in their drinking.
\v 31 \vp 4.1\vp* Thither the animals came that their hearts might delight in the water,
\q1 (Aye), there was Enkidu also, he whom the mountains had gender’d,
\q1 E’en with gazelles did he pasture on herbage, along with the cattle
\v 32 \vp 4.5\vp* Drank he his fill, with the beasts did his heart delight at the water,
\q1 So beheld him the courtesan-girl, the lusty great fellow,
\q1 (O but) a monster (all) savage from out of the depths of the desert!
\q1 “’Tis he, O girl! O, discover thy beauty, thy comeliness shew (him),
\v 33 \vp 4.10\vp* So that thy loveliness he may possess—(O), in no wise be bashful,
\q1 Ravish the soul of him—(certes), as soon as his eye on thee falleth,
\q1 He, forsooth, will approach thee, and thou—O, loosen thy mantle,
\q1 So that he clasp thee, and (then) with the wiles of a woman shalt ply him;
\q1 (Wherefore) his animals, bred in his desert, will (straightway) deny him,
\v 34 \vp 4.15\vp* (Since) to his breast he hath held thee.”
\q1 The girl, displaying her bosom,
\q1 Shew’d him her comeliness, (yea) so that he of her beauty possess’d him,
\q1 Bashful she was not, (but) ravish’d the soul of him, loosing her mantle,
\q1 So that he clasp’d her, (and then) with the wiles of a woman she plied him,
\v 35 \vp 4.20\vp* Holding her unto his breast.
\q1 (’Twas thus that) Enkidu dallied
\q1 Six days, (aye) seven nights, with the courtesan-girl in his mating.
\v 36 \vp 4.22\vp* Sated at length with her charms, he turn’d his face to his cattle,
\q1 O the gazelles, (how) they scamper’d away, as soon as they saw him!
\v 37 \vp 4.25\vp* Him, yea, Enkidu,—fled from his presence the beasts of the desert!
\q1 Enkidu losing his innocence—so, when the cattle fled from him,
\q1 Failed his knees, and he slack’d in his running, (not) as aforetime:
\q1 Natheless he (thus) hath attain’d his full growth and hath broaden’d (his) wisdom.
\v 38 \vp 4.30\vp* Sat he again at the feet of the woman, the woman his features
\q1 Scanning, and, while she was speaking, his ears heard (the words) she was saying:
\q1 “Comely thou art, e’en like to a god, O Enkidu, shalt be,
\v 39 \vp 4.35\vp* Why with the beasts (of the field) dost thou (ever) range over the desert?
\q1 Up! for I’ll lead thee to Erech, the high-wall’d—(in sooth), to the Temple
\q1 Sacred, the dwelling of Anu and Ishtar, where, highest in power,
\q1 Gilgamish is, and prevaileth o’er men like an aurochs.”
\v 40 \vp 4.40\vp* Her counsel
\q1 E’en as she spake it found favour, (for) conscious he was of his longing
\q1 Some companion to seek; so unto the courtesan spake he:
\q1 “Up, then, O girl, to the Temple, the holy (and) sacred, invite me,
\v 41 \vp 4.45\vp* Me, to the dwelling of Anu and Ishtar, where, highest in power,
\q1 Gilgamish is, and prevaileth o’er men like an aurochs—for I, too,
\v 42 \vp 5.1\vp* I, I will summon him, challenging boldly (and) crying through Erech,
\q1 ‘I, too, am mighty!’ Nay, I, forsooth ⌜I⌝, will (e’en) destiny alter—
\q1 (Truly), ’tis he who is born in the desert whose vigour ⌜is greatest!⌝
\q1 . . . . . . . . . . . . . I will ⌜please⌝ thee,
\v 43 \vp 5.5\vp* . . . . . . . . . . . . . ⌜whatever⌝ there be, that would I know.”
\q1 “Enkidu, come (then) to ⌜Erech⌝, the high-wall’d, ⌜where⌝ people ⌜array⌝ them
\q1 ⌜Gorgeous⌝ in festal attire, (and) each day the day is a revel,
\v 44 \vp 5.10\vp* ⌜Eunuch⌝-priests ⌜clashing⌝ (their) cymbals, and ⌜dancing⌝-girls . . . . .
\q1 . . . flown with their wantoning, gleeful, and keeping the nobles
\q1 Out of their beds! (Nay), Enkidu, ⌜joy⌝ in thy life (to its fullest)
\q1 ⌜Thou shalt⌝ taste—(forsooth) will I shew thee a man who is happy,
\v 45 \vp 5.15\vp* Gilgamish! View him, O look on his face, (how) comely his manhood!
\q1 Dower’d with lustiness is he, the whole of his body with power
\q1 Brimming, ⌜his⌝ vigour is stronger than thine, (all) day and night restless!
\v 46 \vp 5.20\vp* Enkidu, temper thine arrogance—Gilgamish, loveth him Shamash,
\q1 Anu, (and) Enlil, and Ea have dower’d his wisdom with largesse.
\v 47 \vp 5.22\vp* (Sooth), or ever from out of thy mountains thou camest, in Erech
\v 48 \vp 5.25\vp* Gilgamish thee had beheld in a dream; so, Gilgamish coming
\q1 Spake to his mother, the dream to reveal.
\q1 ‘O my mother, a vision
\q1 Which I beheld in my night-time. (Behold), there were stars of the heavens,
\q1 When something like unto Anu’s own self fell down on my shoulders,
\v 49 \vp 5.30\vp* (Ah, though) I heaved him, he was o’erstrong for me, (and though) his grapple
\q1 Loosed I, I was unable to shake him (from off me): (and now, all the meanwhile),
\q1 People from Erech were standing about ⌜him, the⌝ artisans ⌜pressing⌝.
\v 50 \vp 5.35\vp* On ⌜him behind⌝, (while) throng’d him ⌜the heroes⌝; my (very) companions
\q1 Kissing ⌜his⌝ feet; ⌜I, I to my breast⌝ like a woman did hold him,
\q1 (Then) ⌜I⌝ presented him low at ⌜thy⌝ feet, ⌜that⌝ as mine own equal.
\q1 ⌜Thou⌝ might’st account him.’
\q1 ⌜She⌝ who knoweth all wisdom (thus) to her Seigneur she answer’d,
\v 51 \vp 5.40\vp* ⌜She⌝ who knoweth all wisdom, to Gilgamish (thus) did she answer:
\q1 ‘(Lo), by the stars of the heavens are represented thy ⌜comrades⌝,
\q1 ⌜That which was like unto⌝ Anu’s ⌜own self⌝, which fell on thy shoulders,
\q1 ⌜Which thou didst heave, but he was⌝ o’erstrong for thee, ⌜(aye), though his grapple
\q1 Thou didst unloose⌝, but to shake him from off thee thou wert ⌜un⌝able,
\v 52 \vp 5.45\vp* ⌜So didst present⌝ him low at my feet, ⌜that⌝ as thine own equal
\q1 ⌜I might⌝ account him—⌜and thou to thy breast like a woman⌝ didst hold him:
\v 53 \vp 6.1\vp* ⌜This is a stoutheart, a⌝ friend, one ready to stand by ⌜a comrade⌝,
\q1 One whose strength ⌜is the greatest, (the length and breadth) of the country⌝,
\q1 ⌜Like to a double of Anu’s own self his⌝ strength is enormous.
\q1 ⌜(Now), since thou⌝ to thy breast didst hold him ⌜the way of a woman⌝,
\v 54 \vp 6.5\vp* ⌜This is a sign that⌝ thou art the one he will ⌜never⌝ abandon:
\q1 ⌜This⌝ of thy dream is the ⌜meaning⌝.’
\q1 ⌜Again he spake⌝ to his mother,
\q1 ‘⌜Mother⌝, a second dream ⌜did I⌝ see: ⌜Into Erech, the high-wall’d⌝,
\v 55 \vp 6.10\vp* Hurtled an axe, and they gather’d about it: ⌜the meanwhile, from Erech⌝
\q1 ⌜People⌝ were standing about it, ⌜the people⌝ (all) thronging before it,
\q1 ⌜Artisans pressing⌝ behind it, ⌜while⌝ I at thy feet did present it,
\v 56 \vp 6.15\vp* ⌜I⌝, like a woman I held it to me ⌜that⌝ thou might’st account it,
\q1 As mine own equal.’
\q1 ⌜She the all⌝-wise, who knoweth all wisdom, (thus) answer’d her offspring,
\q1 ⌜She the all-wise⌝ who knoweth all wisdom, to Gilgamish answer’d:
\q1 ‘(Lo, that) ⌜Axe⌝ thou didst see (is) a Man; like a woman didst hold him,
\v 57 \vp 6.20\vp* Unto thy breast, ⌜that⌝ as thine own equal I might account him,
\q1 ⌜This⌝ is a stoutheart, a friend, one ready to stand by a comrade,
\q1 One whose strength is the ⌜greatest (the length and breadth) of the country⌝,
\q1 ⌜Like to a double of⌝ Anu’s ⌜own self⌝, his strength is enormous.’
\q1 ⌜Gilgamish open’d his mouth, and⌝ addressing his mother, (thus spake he):
\q1 ‘⌜Though⌝ great ⌜danger (?)⌝ befall, ⌜a friend (?)⌝ shall I have . . .’”
\c 2
\cp 2
\p
\v 1 \vp 2.2\vp* While Gilgamish (thus) is the vision revealing
\q1 Enkidu sitteth before the hetaera, and she ⌜displaying her⌝ bosom,
\v 2 \vp 2.5\vp* Shewing ⌜her beauty (?)⌝, the place of his birth he forgetteth.
\q1 (So) Enkidu dallied
\q1 (Thus) for six days, seven nights, with the courtesan-girl in his mating.
\v 3 \vp 2.10\vp* Broke into ⌜speech⌝ then, the nymph, and (thus) unto Enkidu spake she:
\q1 “(Yea, as) I view thee, (e’en) like a god, O Enkidu, shalt be,
\q1 Why with the beasts (of the field) dost thou (ever) range over the desert?
\v 4 \vp 2.15\vp* Up, for I’ll lead thee to ⌜Erech⌝ broad-marketed, (aye), to the Temple
\q1 Sacred, the dwelling of Anu—O Enkidu, come, that I guide thee,
\q1 Unto E-Anna, the dwelling of Anu, where ⌜Gilgamish⌝ (liveth),
\v 5 \vp 2.20\vp* (He), the supreme of creation; and thou, aye, thou wilt ⌜embrace him⌝
\q1 Like ⌜to a woman⌝, (and e’en) ⌜as⌝ thyself thou shalt ⌜love him⌝.
\q1 O, rouse thee
\q1 Up from the ground—’tis a shepherd’s bed (only).”
\q1 Her utterance heard he,
\v 6 \vp 2.25\vp* Welcomed her rede: the advice of the woman struck home in his bosom.
\q1 She one garment took off wherewith she might clothe him: the other
\v 7 \vp 2.30\vp* She herself wore, (and so) taking her hand like a brother she led him
\q1 (Thus) to the booths(?) of the shepherds, the place of the sheepfolds. The shepherds
\v 8 \vp 2.35\vp* Gather⌝d at sight of him
\v 9 \vp 3.1\vp* He (in the past) of the milk of the wild things to suck was accustom’d!
\v 10 \vp 3.5\vp* Bread which she set before him he broke, but he gazed and he stared:
\q1 Enkidu bread did not know how to eat, nor had he the knowledge
\q1 Mead how to quaff!
\v 11 \vp 3.10\vp* (Then) the woman made answer, to Enkidu speaking,
\q1 “Enkidu, taste of the bread, (for) of life ’tis; (forsooth), the essential,
\q1 Drink thou, (too), of the mead, ’tis the wonted use of the country.”
\v 12 \vp 3.15\vp* Enkidu ate of the bread, (aye, ate) until he was gorged,
\q1 Drank of the mead seven bumpers; his spirits rose, (and), exultant,
\v 13 \vp 3.20\vp* Glad was his heart, and cheerful his face: ⌜himself(?)⌝ was he rubbing,
\v 14 \vp 3.25\vp* Oil on the hair of his body anointed: and (thus) became human.
\q1 Donn’d he a garment to be like a man, (and) taking his weapon,
\v 15 \vp 3.30\vp* Hunted the lions, which harried the shepherds o’ nights: and the jackals
\q1 Caught he. (So) he, having master⌝d the lions, the shepherds slept soundly.
\v 16 \vp 3.35\vp* Enkidu—(he) was their warden—(becometh) a man of full vigour.
\q1 (Now) is one of the heroes speaking to ⌜Gilgamish(?)⌝ . . . .
\v 17 \vp 4.10\vp* (Then while) he pleasured, he lifted his eyes, (and), observing the fellow,
\q1 Spake he unto the woman: “O doxy, bring me (this) fellow,
\v 18 \vp 4.15\vp* Why hath he come? I would know his intention.”
\q1 The woman the fellow
\q1 Call’d that he come to him, that he might see him: “O, why art thou seeking,
\q1 Sir? (Pray), which is the way to thy rest-house?”
\v 19 \vp 4.20\vp* The man spake, addressing
\q1 Enkidu: “You to the House of Community ⌜Gilgamish calleth⌝,
\q1 (This is) the custom of men, and a homage (too) to the great ones:
\v 20 \vp 4.25\vp* Come, then, and heap up the offerings such as are due to the city,
\q1 Come, on behalf of the common weal bring in the food of the city.
\q1 (’Tis) for the king of broad-marketed Erech to look on thy greeting,
\v 21 \vp 4.30\vp* Gilgamish, king of broad-marketed Erech to look on thy greeting;
\q1 First doth he mate with the woman allotted by fate, and then after
\q1 Speak by the counsel of god, and so from the shape of the omens
\q1 (Utter the rede of) his destiny.”
\q1 (So) at the words of the fellow
\q1 Went they before him.
\v 22 \vp 5.7\vp* ⌜Enkidu⌝ going ⌜in front⌝, with the courtesan coming behind him,
\v 23 \vp 5.10\vp* Enter’d broad-marketed Erech; the populace gather’d behind him,
\q1 (Then), as he stopp’d in the street of broad-marketed Erech, the people
\v 24 \vp 5.15\vp* Thronging, behind him exclaim’d “Of a truth, like to Gilgamish is he,
\q1 Shorter in stature a trifle, ⌜his⌝ composition is stronger.
\v 25 \vp 5.20\vp* . . . . . . . ⌜(once)⌝ like a ⌜weakling⌝ baby he suck’d the milk of the wild things!
\q1 Ever the bread-cakes in Erech give glorious (climax) to manhood!
\v 26 \vp 5.25\vp* He a (mere) savage becometh a hero of proper appearance,
\q1 (Now) unto Gilgamish, god-like, his composition is equal.”
\v 27 \vp 5.30\vp* Strewn is the couch for the love-rites, and Gilgamish (now) in the night-time
\q1 Cometh to sleep, to delight in the woman: (but) ⌜Enkidu⌝, coming
\v 28 \vp 5.35\vp* (There) in the highway, doth block up the passage to Gilgamish, ⌜threat’ning⌝
\q1 He with his strength . . . . .
\v 29 \vp 6.6\vp* Gilgamish . . . behind him . . . . . . . . . .
\v 30 \vp 6.10\vp* Burgeon’d ⌜his rage⌝, (and) he rush’d to ⌜attack⌝ him: they met in the highway.
\q1 Enkidu barr’d up the door with his foot, (and) to Gilgamish entry
\v 31 \vp 6.15\vp* Would not concede: they grappled and snorted(?) like bulls, (and) the threshold
\q1 Shatter’d: the (very) wall quiver’d as Gilgamish, Enkidu grappled,
\v 32 \vp 6.20\vp* Snorting(?) like bulls, (and) the threshold they shatter’d, the (very) wall quiver’d.
\v 33 \vp 6.25\vp* Gilgamish bent his leg to the ground: (so) his fury abated,
\q1 (Aye, and) his ardour was quell’d: so soon as was quelled his ardour,
\v 34 \vp 6.30\vp* Enkidu (thus) unto Gilgamish spake: “(Of a truth), did thy mother
\q1 Bear thee as one, and one only: (that choicest) cow of the steer-folds,
\v 35 \vp 6.35\vp* Nin-sun, exalted thy head above heroes, and Enlil hath dower’d
\q1 Thee with the kingship o’er men.”
\c 3
\cp 3
\p
\v 1 \vp 2.20\vp* “He lifted up ⌜his foot⌝ to the door . . . . . . . .
\v 2 \vp 2.21\vp* (?) They raged furiously . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
\q1 Enkidu hath not ⌜his equal⌝ . . . unkempt is the hair . . .
\q1 (Aye) he was born in the desert, and ⌜no⌝ one ⌜his presence can equal⌝.”
\v 3 \vp 2.24\vp* Enkidu (there) as he stood gave ear ⌜to his utterance (?)⌝, grieving
\v 4 \vp 2.26\vp* Sitting ⌜in sorrow⌝: his eyes fill’d ⌜with tears⌝, and his arms lost their power,
\q1 ⌜Slack’d was his bodily vigour⌝. Each clasp⌝d ⌜the hand of⌝ the other.
\v 5 \vp 2.37\vp* ⌜Holding⌝ like ⌜brothers⌝ their grip . . . ⌜(and) to Gilgamish⌝ Enkidu answer’d:
\v 6 \vp 2.40\vp* “Friend, ’tis my darling hath circled (her arms) round my neck (to farewell me),
\q1 (Wherefore) my arms lose their power, my bodily vigour is slacken’d.”
\v 7 \vp 2.45\vp* Gilgamish open’d his mouth, and to Enkidu spake he (in this wise):
\v 8 \vp 3.4\vp* “⌜I, O my friend, am determined to go to the Forest of Cedars⌝,
\v 9 \vp 3.5\vp* ⌜(Aye) and⌝ Humbaba the Fierce ⌜will⌝ o’ercome and destroy ⌜what is evil⌝.
\v 10 \vp 3.10\vp* ⌜(Then) will I cut down⌝ the Cedar . . . . .”
\q1 Enkidu open’d his mouth, and to Gilgamish spake he (in this wise),
\v 11 \vp 3.15\vp* “Know, then, my friend, what time I was roaming with kine in the mountains
\q1 I for a distance of two hours’ march from the skirts of the Forest
\q1 Into its depths would go down. Humbaba—his roar was a whirlwind,
\v 12 \vp 3.20\vp* Flame (in) his jaws, and his very breath Death! O, why hast desired
\q1 This to accomplish? To meet(?) with Humbaba were conflict unequall’d.”
\v 13 \vp 3.25\vp* Gilgamish open’d his mouth and to Enkidu spake he (in this wise):
\q1 “⌜Tis that I need⌝ the rich yield of its mountains ⌜I go to the Forest⌝” . . . .
\v 14 \vp 3.36\vp* Enkidu open’d his mouth ⌜and⌝ to Gilgamish spake he (in this wise):
\v 15 \vp 3.40\vp* “(But) when we go to the Forest ⌜of Cedars⌝ . . . its guard is a ⌜Fighter⌝,
\q1 Strong, never ⌜sleeping⌝, O Gilgamish . . . . . .
\v 16 \vp 4.1\vp* So that he safeguard the Forest of Cedars a terror to mortals
\q1 Him hath Enlil appointed—Humbaba, his roar is a whirlwind,
\q1 Flame (in) his jaws, and his very breath Death! (Aye), if he in the Forest.
\q1 Hear (but) a tread(?) on the road—‘Who is this come down to his Forest?’
\q1 So that he safeguard the Forest of Cedars, a terror to mortals,
\q1 Him hath Enlil appointed, and fell hap will seize him who cometh
\q1 Down to his Forest.”
\v 17 \vp 4.3\vp* Gilgamish open’d his mouth and to Enkidu spake he (in this wise):
\v 18 \vp 4.5\vp* “Who, O my friend, is unconquer’d by ⌜death⌝? A divinity, certes,
\q1 Liveth for aye in the daylight, but mortals—their days are (all) number’d,
\q1 All that they do is (but) wind—But to thee, now death thou art dreading,
\v 19 \vp 4.10\vp* Proffereth nothing of substance thy courage—I, I’ll be thy va ward!
\q1 ’Tis thine own mouth shall tell thou didst fear the onslaught (of battle),
\q1 (I, forsooth), if I should fall, my name will have stablish’d (for ever).
\v 20 \vp 4.15\vp* Gilgamish ’twas, who fought with Humbaba, the Fierce!
\q1 (In the future),
\q1 After my children are born to my house, and climb up thee, (saying):
\q1 ‘Tell to us all that thou knowest’ . . . . . .
\v 21 \vp 4.18\vp* ⌜(Yea), when thou⌝ speakest ⌜in this wise⌝, thou grievest my heart (for) the Cedar
\v 22 \vp 4.25\vp* ⌜I am⌝ determined ⌜to fell⌝, that I may gain ⌜fame⌝ everlasting.
\v 23 \vp 4.26\vp* (Now), O my friend, ⌜my charge⌝ to the craftsmen I fain would deliver,
\q1 So that they cast in our presence ⌜our weapons⌝.”
\q1 ⌜The charge⌝ they deliver’d
\v 24 \vp 4.30\vp* Unto the craftsmen: the mould (?) did the workmen prepare, and the axes
\q1 Monstrous they cast: (yea), the celts did they cast, each (weighing) three talents;
\q1 Glaives, (too,) monstrous they cast, with hilts each (weighing) two talents,
\v 25 \vp 4.35\vp* Blades, thirty manas to each, corresponding to fit them: ⌜the inlay(?)⌝,
\q1 Gold thirty manas (each) sword: (so) were Gilgamish, Enkidu laden
\q1 Each with ten talents.
\v 26 \vp 4.38\vp* (And now) ⌜in⌝ the Seven Bolt ⌜Portal of Erech⌝
\q1 Hearing ⌜the bruit(?)⌝ did the artisans gather, ⌜assembled the people(?)⌝,
\v 27 \vp 4.40\vp* (There) in the streets of broad-marketed Erech, ⌜in⌝ Gilgamish’ ⌜honour(?)⌝,
\q1 ⌜So did the Elders of Erech⌝ broad-marketed take seat before him.
\q1 ⌜Gilgamish⌝ spake ⌜thus: “O Elders of Erech⌝ broad-marketed, ⌜hear me!⌝
\v 28 \vp 4.45\vp* ⌜I go against Humbaba, the Fierce, who shall say, when he heareth⌝,
\v 29 \vp 5.1\vp* ‘(Ah), let me look on (this) Gilgamish, he of whom (people) are speaking,
\q1 He with whose fame the countries are fill’d’—’Tis I will o’erwhelm him,
\v 30 \vp 5.5\vp* (There) in the Forest of Cedars—(forsooth), I’ll make the land hear (it)
\q1 (How) like a giant the Scion of Erech is—(yea, for) the Cedars
\q1 I am determined to fell, that I may gain fame everlasting.”
\q1 Gilgamish (thus) did the Elders of Erech broad-marketed answer:
\v 31 \vp 5.10\vp* “Gilgamish, ’tis thou art young, that thy valour (o’ermuch) doth uplift thee,
\q1 Nor dost thou know to the full what thou dost seek to accomplish.
\q1 Unto our ears hath it come of Humbaba, his likeness is twofold.
\v 32 \vp 5.15\vp* Who (of free will) then would ⌜seek to⌝ oppose ⌜in encounter⌝ his weapons?
\q1 Who for a distance of two hours’ march from the skirts of the Forest
\q1 Unto its depths would ⌜go⌝ down? Humbaba, his roar is a whirlwind,
\q1 Flame (in) his jaws, and his very breath Death! (O), why hast desired.
\q1 This to accomplish? To meet(?) with Humbaba were conflict unequall’d.”
\v 33 \vp 5.20\vp* Gilgamish unto the rede of his counsellors hearken’d and ponder’d,
\q1 Cried to ⌜his⌝ friend: “Now, indeed, O ⌜my⌝ friend, ⌜will I⌝ thus ⌜voice opinion⌝.
\q1 I (forsooth) dread him, and (yet) to ⌜(the depths of the) Forest⌝ I’ll take ⌜me⌝ . .”
\v 34 \vp 5.23\vp* “ . . . . . . . . may thy god (so) ⌜protect⌝ thee,
\q1 Bringing thee back ⌜(safe and)⌝ sound to the walls of ⌜broad-marketed⌝ Erech.”
\v 35 \vp 5.35\vp* Gilgamish knelt ⌜before Shamash⌝ a word ⌜in his presence⌝ to utter:
\q1 “Here I present myself, Shamash, ⌜to lift up⌝ my hands (in entreaty),
\q1 O that hereafter my life may be spared, to the ramparts of ⌜Erech⌝
\v 36 \vp 5.40\vp* Bring me again: spread thine aegis ⌜upon me⌝ .”
\q1 And Shamash made answer,
\q1 ⌜Speaking⌝ his oracle . . . . . . .
\v 37 \vp 6.1\vp* Tears adown Gilgamish’ ⌜cheeks were (now)⌝ streaming: “A road I have never
\q1 Traversed ⌜I go, on a passage(?)⌝ I know not, (but if) I be spared
\v 38 \vp 6.5\vp* (So) in content ⌜will I⌝ come ⌜and will pay thee(?)⌝ due meed (?) of thy homage.”
\v 39 \vp 6.10\vp* Monstrous ⌜the axes they brought(?)⌝, they deliver’d ⌜the bow⌝ and the quiver
\q1 ⌜Into⌝ (his) hand; (so) taking a celt, ⌜he slung on (?)⌝ his quiver,
\v 40 \vp 6.15\vp* ⌜Grasping⌝ another ⌜celt(?) he fasten’d his glaive⌝ to his baldrick.
\q1 ⌜But, or ever the twain⌝ had set forth on their journey, they offer’d
\q1 ⌜Gifts⌝ to the Sun-god, that home he might bring them to Erech (in safety).
\v 41 \vp 6.20\vp* (Now) do the ⌜Elders⌝ farewell him with blessings, to Gilgamish giving
\q1 Counsel ⌜concerning⌝ the road: “O Gilgamish, to thine own power
\q1 Trust not (alone); (but at least) let thy ⌜road⌝ be traversed ⌜before⌝ thee,
\q1 Guard thou thy person; let Enkidu go before thee (as vaward).
\q1 (Aye, for) ’twas he hath discover’d the ⌜way⌝, the road he hath travell’d.
\v 42 \vp 6.25\vp* (Sooth), of the Forest the passes are all under sway (?) ⌜of⌝ Humbaba,
\q1 ⌜(Yea), he who goeth⌝ as vaward is (able) to safeguard a comrade,
\q1 O that the Sun-god ⌜may grant⌝ thee ⌜success to attain⌝ thine ⌜ambition⌝,
\v 43 \vp 6.30\vp* O that he grant that thine eyes see (consummate) the words of thy utt’rance
\q1 O that he level the path that is block’d, cleave a road for thy treading,
\v 44 \vp 6.35\vp* Cleave, too, the berg for thy foot! May the god Lugal-banda
\q1 Bring in thy night-time a message to thee, with which shalt be gladden’d,
\q1 So that it help thine ambition, (for), like a boy thine ambition
\q1 On the o’erthrow of Humbaba thou fixest, as thou hast settled.
\v 45 \vp 6.40\vp* Wash, (then), thy feet: when thou haltest, shalt hollow a pool, so that ever
\q1 Pure be the water within thy skin-bottle, (aye), cool be the water
\q1 Unto the Sun-god thou pourest, (and thus) shalt remind Lugal-banda.”
\v 46 \vp 6.45\vp* Enkidu open’d his mouth, and spake unto Gilgamish, (saying):
\q1 “⌜Gilgamish⌝, art (?) thou (in truth) full equal to making (this) foray?
\q1 Let ⌜not⌝ thy heart be afraid; trust me.”
\q1 On (his) shoulder his mantle
\v 47 \vp 6.50\vp* ⌜Drew⌝ he, (and now) ⌜on the road⌝ to Humbaba they set forth (together).
\v 48 \vp 6.56\vp* “ . . . they went with me . . . ⌜tell⌝ you . . in joy of heart.”
\v 49 \vp 6.60\vp* ⌜So when he heard⌝ this his word, the man on his way did ⌜direct him⌝:
\q1 “Gilgamish, go, . . . let thy brother (?) precede ⌜thee⌝ . . . ⌜(and) in thine ambition⌝.
\q1 ⌜O that the Sun-god (?)⌝ may shew ⌜thee⌝ success!”
\v 50 \vp 6b.2\vp* “Gilgamish, put not thy faith in the strength of thine own person (solely),
\q1 Quench’d be thy wishes to trusting(?) (o’ermuch) in thy (shrewdness in) smiting.
\q1 (Sooth), he who goeth as vaward is able to safeguard a comrade,
\v 51 \vp 6b.5\vp* He who doth know how to guide hath guarded his friend; (so) before thee,
\q1 Do thou let Enkidu go, (for ’tis) he to the Forest of Cedars
\q1 Knoweth the road: ’tis he lusteth for battle, and threateneth combat.
\q1 Enkidu—he would watch over a friend, would safeguard a comrade,
\v 52 \vp 6b.10\vp* (Aye, such an one) would deliver his person from out of the pitfalls.
\q1 We, O King, in our conclave have paid deep heed to thy welfare,
\q1 Thou, O King, in return with an (equal) heed shalt requite us.”
\q1 Gilgamish open’d his mouth, and spake unto Enkidu, saying:
\v 53 \vp 6b.15\vp* “Unto the Palace of Splendour, O friend, come, let us betake us,
\q1 Unto the presence of Nin-sun, the glorious Queen, (aye) to Nin-sun,
\q1 Wisest of (all) clever women, all-knowing; a well-devised pathway
\q1 She will prescribe for our feet.”
\v 54 \vp 6b.20\vp* Clasp’d they their hands, each to each, and went to the Palace of Splendour,
\q1 Gilgamish, Enkidu. Unto the glorious Queen, (aye) to Nin-sun
\q1 Gilgamish came, and he enter’d in unto ⌜the presence of Nin-sun⌝:
\q1 “Nin-sun, O fain would I tell thee ⌜how⌝ I a far journey ⌜am going⌝,
\v 55 \vp 6b.25\vp* (Unto) the home ⌜of Humbaba to counter a⌝ warfare I know not,
\q1 ⌜Follow a road⌝ which I ⌜know⌝ not, ⌜(aye) from the time of my starting⌝,
\q1 ⌜Till my return, until I arrive at the Forest of Cedars⌝,
\q1 ⌜Till I o’erthrow Humbaba, the Fierce, and destroy from the country⌝
\q1 ⌜All that the Sun-god abhorreth of evil⌝” . . . .
\v 56 \vp 6b.30\vp* “ . . . garb thyself; . . . in thy presence.”
\q1 (So) to her offspring, to Gilgamish ⌜Nin-sun⌝ gave ear . . . -ly,
\v 57 \vp 2b.1\vp* Enter’d ⌜her chamber⌝ . . . ⌜and deck’d herself⌝ with the flowers of Tulal(?),
\q1 ⌜Put on⌝ the festal garb of her body . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
\v 58 \vp 2b.5\vp* ⌜Put on⌝ the festal garb of her bosom . . ., her head ⌜with a circlet⌝
\q1 Crown’d, and . . . the ground ipirani.
\q1 Climb’d ⌜she the stairway⌝, ascended the roof, and ⌜the parapet(?)⌝ mounted,
\q1 Offer’d her incense to Shamash, (her) sacrifice offer’d ⌜to Shamash⌝,
\q1 (Then) towards Shamash her hands she uplifted (in orison saying):
\v 59 \vp 2b.10\vp* “Why didst thou give (this) restlessness of spirit
\q1 With which didst dower Gilgamish, ⌜my⌝ son?
\q1 That now thou touchest him, and (straight) he starteth
\q1 A journey far to where Humbaba (dwelleth),
\q1 To counter warfare which he knoweth not,
\q1 Follow a pathway which he knoweth not,
\v 60 \vp 2b.15\vp* (Aye), from the very day on which he starteth,
\q1 Till he return, till to the Cedar Forest
\q1 He reach; till he o’erthrow the fierce Humbaba,
\q1 And from the land destroy all evil things
\q1 Which thou abhor’st; the day which ⌜thou hast set⌝
\v 61 \vp 2b.20\vp* As term, of (that) strong man (who) feareth thee,
\q1 May Aa, (thy) bride, be ⌜thy⌝ remembrancer.
\q1 He the night-watches . . . . .”
\v 62 \vp 2c.9\vp* “(Aye, such an one) ⌜would deliver his person⌝ from out of the pitfalls.
\v 63 \vp 2c.10\vp* ⌜We, O King⌝, in our conclave ⌜have paid deep heed to thy welfare⌝,
\q1 ⌜(Now), O King, in thy turn with an (equal) heed⌝ shalt requite us.”
\q1 Enkidu ⌜open’d⌝ his mouth ⌜and spake unto Gilgamish, saying⌝:
\q1 “Turn, O my friend . . . . a road not . . . .”
\c 4
\cp 4
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.36\vp* Enkidu lifted ⌜his eyes⌝ . . . and spake with the Gate as ⌜’t were human(?)⌝:
\q1 “O thou Gate of the Forest without understanding(?) . . .
\q1 Sentience which thou hast not, . . . .
\v 2 \vp 1.40\vp* I for (full) forty leagues have admired thy ⌜wonderful⌝ timber,
\q1 (Aye), till I sighted the towering Cedar . . . .
\q1 (O but) thy wood hath no peer (in the country) . . .
\q1 Six gar thy height, and two gar thy breadth . . .
\v 3 \vp 1.45\vp* (Sooth, but) thy stanchion (?), thy socket (?), thy pivot (?), thy lock (?), and thy shutter (?),
\q1 ⌜(All of them) must have been fashion’d for thee⌝ in the City of Nippur!
\q1 O, if I had but known, O Gate, that this was ⌜thy grandeur⌝,
\q1 This, too, the grace ⌜of thy structure⌝, then either an axe had I lifted
\v 4 \vp 1.50\vp* Or I had . . . or bound together . . . .”
\v 5 \vp 1b.6\vp* “. . . ⌜O, haste⌝ thee, withstand him, he will not ⌜pursue(?) thee⌝,
\q1 ⌜We will⌝ go on down into the wood not ⌜daunted, together (?)⌝.
\q1 . . . Thou shall put on seven garments ..
\q1 . . . putting on, and six . . . (?) . . . ”
\v 6 \vp 1b.10\vp* He like a mighty wild bull . . .
\q1 Flung he the Portal afar, and ⌜his⌝ mouth was fill’d (with his challenge),
\q1 Cried to the Guard of the Forest: “Up (?) . . . !
\q1 ⌜’Tis I will challenge⌝ Humbaba like to a . . .”
\v 7 \vp 6.1\vp* “Trouble (?) ⌜I foresee(?)⌝ wherever I go . . . .
\v 8 \vp 6.5\vp* O my friend, I have ⌜seen⌝ a dream which un- . . . .”
\q1 The day of the dream he had seen fulfilled . . . .
\v 9 \vp 6.7\vp* Enkidu lay for a day, ⌜yea, a second⌝—for Enkidu ⌜lying⌝
\v 10 \vp 6.10\vp* (Prone) on his couch, was a third and a fourth day . . ., a fifth, sixth and seventh,
\q1 Eighth, ninth, ⌜and tenth⌝. While Enkidu ⌜lay in his⌝ sickness . . ., th’ eleventh,
\q1 (Aye, till) the twelfth . . . on ⌜his⌝ couch was Enkidu ⌜lying⌝.
\v 11 \vp 6.15\vp* Call’d he to Gilgamish, . . . . . . . . .
\q1 “(O but), my comrade, . . . hateth me . . because within Erech
\q1 I was afraid of the combat, and . . . My friend, who in battle . . .”
\v 12 \vp 6.26\vp* ⌜Enkidu open’d⌝ his ⌜mouth⌝ and spake ⌜unto Gilgamish, saying⌝:
\q1 (“Nay, but), ⌜my friend, let us no wise⌝ go down ⌜to the depths of the Forest⌝,
\q1 (For) ’tis my hands ⌜have grown weak⌝, and ⌜my arms⌝ are stricken with palsy.”
\v 13 \vp 6.29\vp* Gilgamish open’d his mouth and spake ⌜unto Enkidu⌝, saying:
\v 14 \vp 6.30\vp* “Shall we, O friend, ⌜play⌝ the coward? . . . . . .
\q1 . . . . thou shalt surpass them all(?) . . . .
\q1 ⌜Thou, O⌝ my friend, art cunning in warfare, art ⌜shrewd(?)⌝ in the battle,
\q1 (So) shalt thou touch the . . . and of ⌜death⌝ have no terror,
\v 15 \vp 6.34\vp* ⌜So that⌝ the palsy (now striking) thine arms ⌜may⌝ depart, and the weakness
\q1 Pass ⌜from thy hands⌝! ⌜Be brave(?)⌝ and resist! O my comrade, together
\q1 We will go down—let the combat ⌜in no wise diminish(?)⌝ thy courage!
\v 16 \vp 6.40\vp* O forget death, and be fearful(?) of nothing(?) . . (for he who is) ⌜valiant(?)⌝,
\q1 Cautious (and) careful, by leading ⌜the way⌝ hath his own body guarded,
\q1 (He ’tis) will safeguard a comrade.”
\q1 A name by their ⌜valour(?)⌝ . .
\q1 They will establish. (And now) they together arrive at the barrier(?),
\q1 ⌜Still’d into silence(?)⌝ their speech, and they themselves (suddenly) stopping.
\c 5
\cp 5
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* Stood they and stared at the Forest, they gazed at the height of the Cedars,
\q1 Scanning the avenue into the Forest: (and there) where Humbaba
\v 2 \vp 1.5\vp* Stalk’d, was a path, (and) straight were his tracks, and good was the passage.
\q1 (Eke) they beheld the Mount of the Cedar, the home of th’ Immortals,
\q1 Shrine ⌜of Irnini, the Cedar uplifting its pride ’gainst the mountain,
\q1 Fair was its shade, (all) full of delight, with bushes (there) spreading,
\q1 Spread, too, the . . . . the Cedar the incense . . . .
\v 3 \vp 2.32\vp* “⌜Then came another dream to me, comrade, and this second⌝ vision
\q1 ⌜Pleasant, indeed⌝, which I saw, (for) we (?) ⌜twain were standing together⌝
\q1 ⌜High on (?) a⌝ peak of the mountains, ⌜and then did the mountain peak⌝ topple,
\v 4 \vp 2.35\vp* ⌜Leaving us twain (?)⌝ to be like . . . (?) which are born in the desert.”
\q1 Enkidu spake to his comrade the dream (?) ⌜to interpret⌝, (thus saying):
\q1 “Comrade, (in sooth, this) vision ⌜of thine unto us⌝ good fortune (forbodeth),
\q1 (Aye), ’tis a dream of great gain ⌜thou didst see⌝, (for, bethink you), O comrade,
\v 5 \vp 2.40\vp* (Surely) the mountain which thou hast beholden ⌜must needs be Humbaba(?)⌝.
\q1 (Thus doth it mean) we shall capture Humbaba, (and) ⌜throw down his⌝ carcase,
\q1 ⌜Leaving⌝ his corpse in abasement—to-morrow’s (outcome) will I ⌜shew thee⌝”.
\v 6 \vp 2.43\vp* (Now) at the fortieth league did they break their fast ⌜with a morsel⌝,
\v 7 \vp 2.45\vp* (Now) at the sixtieth rested, and hollow’d a pit in the sunshine . . .
\q1 Gilgamish mounted above ⌜it⌝ . . . (and) pour’d out his meal ⌜for the mountain⌝:
\v 8 \vp 2.50\vp* “Mountain, a dream do thou grant . . . breathe on him . . .”
\v 9 \vp 3.1\vp* Granted ⌜the mountain⌝ a dream . . . it breathed on him . . .
\q1 Then a chill wind-blast ⌜up⌝-sprang (and) ⌜a gust⌝ passing over . . .
\v 10 \vp 3.5\vp* ⌜Made⌝ him to cower, and . . . ⌜thereat he sway’d⌝ like the corn of the mountains . . .
\q1 Gilgamish, ⌜squatting⌝ bent-kneed, supported his haunches, (and straightway)
\q1 Sleep (such as) floweth on man descended upon him: ⌜at⌝ midnight
\q1 Ending his slumber (all sudden), he hied him to speak to his comrade:
\v 11 \vp 3.10\vp* “Didst thou not call me, O friend? (O), why am I waken’d (from slumber)?
\q1 Didst thou not touch me—(for), why am I fearful(?), (or) hath not some spirit
\q1 Pass’d (me)? (Or,) why is my flesh (all) a-quiver?
\v 12 \vp 3.13\vp* A third dream, O comrade,
\q1 I have beheld: but all awesome (this) dream which I have beholden:
\v 13 \vp 3.15\vp* (Loud) did the firmament roar, (and) earth (with the echo) resounded,
\q1 Sombre the day, with darkness uprising, (and) levin bolts flashing,
\q1 Kindled were flames, ⌜and there, too, was Pestilence (?)⌝ fill’d to o’erflowing,
\q1 Gorgéd was Death! (Then) ⌜faded⌝ the glare, (then) faded the fires,
\v 14 \vp 3.20\vp* Falling, ⌜the brands⌝ turn’d to ashes—⌜Come, let us go⌝ down to the desert,
\q1 That we may counsel together.”
\q1 Enkidu (now) to interpret his dream unto Gilgamish speaketh:
\v 15 \vp 6.1\vp* In the following manner . . . the Sun-god in heaven . . . the trees:
\q1 He saw ⌜Gilgamish⌝: of the Sun-god in heaven in . . .
\v 16 \vp 6.5\vp* And ⌜shew’d him⌝ the dam on the ditches.
\q1 Gilgamish ⌜spake⌝ then ⌜in orison⌝ unto the Sun-god in heaven;
\q1 “Lo, on that day to the city . . . . which is in the city:
\v 17 \vp 6.10\vp* I in sooth ⌜pray⌝ to the Sun-god in heaven: I on a road have now started,
\q1 . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .”
\q1 Unto th’ entreaty of Gilgamish hearken’d the Sun-god in heaven,
\q1 Wherefore against Humbaba he raised mighty winds: (yea), a great wind,
\q1 Wind from the North, (aye), ⌜a wind from the South⌝, yea ⌜a tempest⌝ (and) storm wind,
\v 18 \vp 6.15\vp* Chill wind, (and) whirlwind, a wind of (all) evil: ’twas eight winds he raiséd,
\q1 Seizing ⌜Humbaba⌝ before and behind, so that nor to go forwards,
\q1 Nor to go back was he able: and then Humbaba surrender’d.
\v 19 \vp 6.20\vp* Wherefore to Gilgamish spake (thus) Humbaba: “O Gilgamish, (pr’y thee),
\q1 Stay, (now, thy hand): be ⌜thou⌝ now my ⌜master⌝, and I’ll be thy henchman:
\q1 ⌜O disregard⌝ (all) ⌜the words⌝ which I spake ⌜(so) boastful⌝ against thee,
\v 20 \vp 6.25\vp* Weighty . . . I would lay me down . . . and the Palace.”
\q1 Thereat to ⌜Gilgamish⌝ Enkidu ⌜spake⌝: “⌜Of the rede which⌝ Humbaba
\q1 ⌜Maketh to thee⌝ thou darest in nowise offer acceptance.
\q1 (Aye, for) Humbaba ⌜must⌝ not ⌜remain alive⌝ . . . .”
\v 21 \vp 6.29\vp* . . . . ⌜they cut off⌝ the head of Humbaba.
\c 6
\cp 6
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* (Now) is he washing his stains, (and) is cleansing his garments in tatters,
\q1 Braiding (?) (the locks of) his hair (to descend loose) over his shoulders,
\q1 Laying aside his garments besmirchen, (and) donning his clean ones,
\q1 Putting on armlets (?), and girding his body about with a baldric,
\v 2 \vp 1.5\vp* Gilgamish bindeth his fillet, and girdeth himself with a baldric.
\v 3 \vp 1.6\vp* (Now) Lady Ishtar espieth the beauty of Gilgamish: (saith she),
\q1 “Gilgamish, come, be a bridegroom, to me of the fruit (of thy body)
\q1 Grant me largesse: (for) my husband shalt be and I’ll be thy consort.
\v 4 \vp 1.10\vp* O, but I’ll furnish a chariot for thee, (all) azure and golden,
\q1 Golden its wheel, and its yoke precious stones, each day to be harness’d
\q1 Unto great mules: (O), enter our house with the fragrance of cedar.
\v 5 \vp 1.15\vp* (So) when thou enterest into our house shall threshold and dais
\q1 Kiss thy feet, (and) beneath thee do homage kings, princes, and rulers,
\q1 Bringing thee yield of the mountains and plains as a tribute: thy she-goats
\q1 Bring forth in plenty, thy ewes shall bear twins, thy asses attaining
\v 6 \vp 1.20\vp* (Each) to the size of a mule, (and) thy steeds in thy chariot winning
\q1 Fame for their gallop: ⌜thy mules⌝ in the yoke shall ne’er have a rival.”
\v 7 \vp 1.22\vp* ⌜Gilgamish⌝ open’d his mouth in reply, Lady Ishtar ⌜to answer⌝:
\q1 “⌜Aye, but what must I give⌝ thee, (if (?)) I should take thee in marriage?
\v 8 \vp 1.25\vp* ⌜I must provide thee with oil⌝ for (thy) body, and clothing: (aye, also)
\q1 ⌜Give thee (thy)⌝ bread and (thy) victual: (sooth), must be sustenance ⌜ample⌝
\q1 Meet for divinity—⌜I, (too), must give thee (thy) drink⌝ fit for royalty.
\v 9 \vp 1.30\vp* . . . . I shall be bound, . . . let us amass (?), . . . clothe with a garment.
\q1 ⌜What, then, will be my advantage, supposing⌝ I take thee in marriage?
\q1 ⌜Thou’rt but a ruin which giveth no shelter (?) to man⌝ from the weather,
\q1 Thou’rt but a back door ⌜not⌝ giving resistance to blast or to windstorm,
\v 10 \vp 1.35\vp* Thou’rt but a palace which dasheth the heroes ⌜within it to pieces⌝,
\q1 Thou’rt but a pitfall (which letteth) its covering ⌜give way (all treach’rous)⌝,
\q1 Thou art but pitch which ⌜defileth⌝ the man who doth carry it with him,
\q1 Thou’rt but a bottle which ⌜leaketh⌝ on him who doth carry it with him,
\q1 Thou art but limestone which ⌜letteth⌝ stone ramparts ⌜fall crumbling in ruin⌝.
\v 11 \vp 1.40\vp* Thou’rt but chalcedony ⌜failing to guard (?)⌝ in an enemy’s country,
\q1 Thou’rt but a sandal which causeth its owner ⌜to trip (by the wayside)⌝.
\q1 Who was ever ⌜thy⌝ husband ⌜thou faithfully lovedst⌝ for all time?
\q1 Who hath been ever thy lord who hath gain’d ⌜over thee⌝ the advantage?
\q1 Come, and I will unfold thee ⌜the endless tale⌝ of thy husbands.
\v 12 \vp 1.45\vp* (Sooth), thou shalt vouch (?) for the truth (?) of (this) list—Thy maidenhood’s consort,
\q1 Tammuz, each year dost make him the cause of Wailing, (then cometh
\q1 Next) the bird Roller gay-feather’d thou lovedst, and (yet) thou didst smite him
\v 13 \vp 1.50\vp* Breaking his wing: in the grove doth he stand, crying kappi ‘my wing!’
\q1 Lovedst thou also a Lion, in (all) the full strength of (his) vigour,
\q1 (Yet) thou didst dig for him seven and seven (deep) pits (to entrap him).
\q1 Lovedst thou also a Stallion, magnificent he in the battle,
\q1 Thou wert the cause of a bridle, a spur, and a whip to him: (also)
\v 14 \vp 1.55\vp* Thou wert the cause of his fifty miles galloping; thou wert the cause, too,
\q1 (Eke), of exhaustion and sweating (?); (thereafter), ’twas thou who didst (also)
\q1 Unto his mother Silili give cause for (her deep) lamentation.
\q1 Lovedst thou also a Shepherd, a neatherd, for thee without ceasing
\v 15 \vp 1.60\vp* Each day to sacrifice yeanlings for thee would heap thee his charcoal,
\q1 (Yet) thou didst smite him, transforming him into a jackal: his herd boy
\q1 Yea, his own herd boy drove him away, and his dogs tore his buttocks.
\q1 Lovedst thou, too, Ishullanu, the gardener he of thy sire,
\v 16 \vp 1.65\vp* Bringing delights (?) to thee ceaseless, while daily he garnish’d thy platter;
\q1 ’Twas for thee only to cast thine eyes on him, and with him be smitten.
\q1 ‘O Ishullanu of mine, come, let me taste of thy vigour,
\q1 Put forth thy hand, too, . . . . . . . . . . ‘
\v 17 \vp 1.70\vp* But he, Ishullanu,
\q1 Said to thee ‘What dost thou ask me? Save only my mother hath baked (it),
\q1 Nought have I eaten—(and) what I should eat would be bread of transgression,
\q1 (Aye) and iniquity! (Further), the reeds are a cloak against winter.’
\v 18 \vp 1.75\vp* Thou this ⌜his answer⌝ didst hear, didst smite him and make him a spider(?),
\q1 Making him lodge midway up a ⌜dwelling(?)⌝—not to move upwards
\q1 Lest there be drainage; nor down, lest a crushing ⌜o’erwhelm him⌝.
\q1 So, too, me in my turn thou wouldst love and (then) ⌜reckon⌝ me like them.”
\v 19 \vp 1.80\vp* ⌜Heard⌝ this (then) Ishtar: she burst into rage and ⌜went up⌝ to Heaven,
\q1 Hied her (thus) Ishtar to Anu, ⌜her father⌝, to Antu, her mother,
\v 20 \vp 1.85\vp* Came she ⌜to tell (them)⌝: “O father, doth Gilgamish load me with insult,
\q1 Gilgamish tale of my sins, my sins and iniquities telleth.”
\v 21 \vp 1.87\vp* Anu made answer, (thus) speaking, and said unto Ishtar the Lady:
\q1 “Nay, thou didst ask him ⌜to grant thee largesse of the fruit of his body⌝,
\v 22 \vp 1.90\vp* (Hence) he the tale of thy sins, thy sins and iniquities telleth.”
\v 23 \vp 1.91\vp* Ishtar made answer (thus) speaking, and said unto ⌜Anu, her father⌝:
\q1 “Father, O make (me) a Heavenly Bull, which shall Gilgamish ⌜vanquish⌝,
\v 24 \vp 1.95\vp* Filling ⌜its body⌝ with flame . . . .
\q1 But if thou’lt ⌜not⌝ make ⌜this Bull⌝, then . . . .
\q1 I’ll smite . . . ., I’ll put . . . ., I’ll . . . .
\v 25 \vp 1.100\vp* More than the . . . will be the . . . . . . .
\v 26 \vp 1.101\vp* Anu ⌜made answer, (thus) speaking, and said unto⌝ Ishtar, the Lady:
\q1 “⌜If I the Heavenly Bull shall create, for which⌝ thou dost ask me,
\q1 (Then) seven years of (leer) husks ⌜must needs follow after his onslaught (?)⌝.
\v 27 \vp 1.105\vp* Wilt thou ⌜for man⌝ gather ⌜corn (?)⌝, and increase ⌜for the cattle(?)⌝ the fodder (?).”
\v 28 \vp 1.106\vp* ⌜Ishtar made⌝ answer, (thus) speaking ⌜and said unto⌝ Anu, her father:
\q1 “⌜Corn for mankind⌝ have I hoarded, have grown ⌜for the cattle the fodder⌝,
\v 29 \vp 1.110\vp* ⌜If seven⌝ years of (leer) husks ⌜must needs follow after his onslaught (?)⌝
\q1 ⌜I will for man⌝ gather ⌜corn and increase for the cattle⌝ the fodder.”
\v 30 \vp 1.130\vp* Enkidu girded (?) his middle; (and straightway) Enkidu, leaping,
\q1 Seized on the Heavenly Bull by ⌜his⌝ horns, and (headlong) before him
\q1 Cast down the Heavenly Bull his full length, . . . .
\q1 (Aye), by the thick of his tail.
\v 31 \vp 1.147\vp* Chased him did Enkidu, . . . the Heavenly Bull . . .
\q1 Seized him and by ⌜the thick⌝ of his tail . . . .
\v 32 \vp 1.153\vp* (So), what time they the Bull of the Heavens had kill’d, its heart they removéd,
\q1 Unto the Sun-god they offer’d in sacrifice; when the libation
\v 33 \vp 1.155\vp* Unto the Sun they had voided, they sate them down, the two brothers.
\v 34 \vp 1.156\vp* (Then) mounted Ishtar (the crest of) the ramparts of Erech, the high-wall’d,
\q1 (So) to the roof-top ascended, (and there) gave voice to her wailing;
\v 35 \vp 1.160\vp* “Woe unto Gilgamish—he who by killing the Bull of the Heavens,
\q1 Made me lament.” When Enkidu heard this, the shrieking of Ishtar,
\q1 Wrenching the member from out of the Bull, he toss’d (it) before her;
\v 36 \vp 1.165\vp* “If I could only have reach’d thee, i’faith, I’d ha’ served thee the same way,
\q1 I’d ha’ let dangle his guts on thy flanks (as a girdle about thee).”
\q1 Ishtar assembled the girl-devotees, the hetaerae and harlots,
\q1 Over the member (torn out) from the Bull she led the lamenting.
\v 37 \vp 1.170\vp* Gilgamish call’d to the masters of craft, the artists, (yea), all of them,
\q1 That at the size of its horns (all) the guilds of the crafts speak their praises
\q1 Each had of azure in weight thirty minas to be as their setting,
\q1 Two fingers their . ... ... . . . . . . . . . . .
\v 38 \vp 1.175\vp* Both of them held six measures of oil; to his god Lugal-banda
\q1 He for (his) unguent devoting, brought in, and (thus) let them hang (there),
\q1 (There) in the shrine of his forbears.
\q1 (And now) in the River Euphrates
\q1 Washing their hands, they start (on their progress) and come (to the city);
\q1 (Now) are they striding the highway of Erech, the heroes of Erech
\v 39 \vp 1.180\vp* Thronging (about them) to see them. (Then) Gilgamish utter’d a riddle
\q1 Unto the notables (?):
\v 40 \vp 1.182\vp* Who, pr’ythee, is most splendid of heroes,
\q1 Who, pr’ythee, is most famous of giants?
\q1 Gilgamish—he is most splendid of heroes,
\v 41 \vp 1.185\vp* ⌜Enkidu—he is most⌝ famous of giants.
\v 42 \vp 1.190\vp* So in his palace did Gilgamish hold high revel: (thereafter),
\q1 (While all) the heroes asleep, on their nightly couches were lying
\q1 Enkidu, too, was asleep, and a vision beheld, and (so) coming
\q1 Enkidu (now) his dream to reveal: (thus) spake he unto his comrade.
\c 7
\cp 7
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* “Why, O my friend, do the great gods (now) take counsel together?”
\v 2 \vp 1.30\vp* “Of want . . . . since me it is that . . .hath . . . .
\q1 And me the fever ⌜hath laid⌝ on my back.”
\v 3 \vp 1.32\vp* Heard him the Sun-god, and open’d his mouth, and from out of the heavens
\q1 (Straightway) he call’d him: “O Enkidu, why dost thou curse the hetaera?
\v 4 \vp 1.35\vp* She ’twas who made thee eat bread, for divinity proper: (aye), wine (too),
\q1 She made thee drink, (’twas) for royalty proper: a generous mantle
\q1 Put on thee, (aye), and for comrade did give to thee Gilgamish splendid.
\v 5 \vp 1.40\vp* Now on a couch of great size will he, (thy) friend (and) thy brother
\q1 Gilgamish, grant thee to lie, on a handsome couch will he grant thee
\q1 Rest, and to sit on a throne of great ease, a throne at (his) left hand,
\q1 So that the princes of Hades may kiss thy feet (in their homage);
\q1 He, too, will make (all) the people of Erech lament in thy (honour),
\v 6 \vp 1.45\vp* Making them mourn thee, (and) damsels (and) heroes constrain to thy service,
\q1 ⌜While he himself⌝ for thy sake will cause his body to carry
\q1 Stains, ⌜(and) will put on⌝ the skin of a lion, and range o’er the desert.”
\v 7 \vp 1.48\vp* Enkidu ⌜(then) giving ear⌝ to the words of the valiant Shamash
\q1 Speaking . . . . . . . . . his wrath was appeased.
\v 8 \vp 4.1\vp* “ . . . . . . . . . . . may . . . restore to thy place!
\q1 ⌜(So, too), may monarchs and princes⌝ and chiefs be with love ⌜for thee⌝ smitten;
\q1 ⌜None smite (?)⌝ his breech ⌜in disgust (?); against thee; and for thee may the hero⌝
\q1 Comb out his locks; . . . who would embrace ⌜thee⌝,
\v 9 \vp 4.5\vp* Let him his girdle unloose . . . and thy ⌜bed⌝ be azure and golden;
\q1 May . . . entreat thee kindly (?), . . . . are heap’d his ishshikku
\q1 May the gods make thee enter . . . . . . . . . . .
\v 10 \vp 4.10\vp* ⌜Mayst thou⌝ be left as the mother of seven brides . . .”
\v 11 \vp 4.11\vp* ⌜Enkidu⌝ . . . woe in his belly . . . sleeping alone,
\q1 ⌜Came⌝ in the night ⌜to discover⌝ his heaviness unto his comrade:
\q1 “⌜Friend⌝, (O) a dream I have seen in my night-time: the firmament ⌜roaring⌝,
\v 12 \vp 4.15\vp* Echo’d the earth, and I ⌜by myself⌝ was standing(?) . . .
\q1 ⌜When perceived I a man (?)⌝, (all) dark was his face, ⌜and⌝ was liken’d
\q1 ⌜Unto⌝ . . .his face, . . . ⌜and⌝ his nails like claws of a lion.
\v 13 \vp 4.20\vp* Me did he overcome . . . climbing up . . . press’d me down,
\q1 Upon me . . . my (?) body . . . . . .
\v 14 \vp 4.33\vp* . . . . . . . . like birds my hands: (and) he seized (?) me,
\q1 Me did he lead to the Dwelling of Darkness, the home of Irkalla,
\v 15 \vp 4.35\vp* Unto the Dwelling from which he who entereth cometh forth never!
\q1 (Aye), by the road on the passage whereof there can be no returning,
\q1 Unto the Dwelling whose tenants are (ever) bereft of the daylight,
\q1 Where for their food is the dust, and the mud is their sustenance: bird-like
\v 16 \vp 4.40\vp* Wear they a garment of feathers: and, sitting (there) in the darkness,
\q1 Never the light will they see. On the Gate . . . . when I enter’d
\q1 On the house (?) . . . . was humbled the crown,
\q1 For . . . those who (wore) crowns, who of old ruled over the country,
\q1 . . . . of Anu and Enlil ’twas they set the bakemeats,
\v 17 \vp 4.45\vp* Set . . . ., cool was the water they served from the skins. When I enter’d
\q1 Into (this) House of the Dust, were High Priest and acolyte sitting,
\q1 Seer and magician, the priest who the Sea of the great gods anointed,
\q1 (Here) sat Etana, Sumuqan; the Queen of the Underworld (also),
\q1 Ereshkigal, in whose presence doth bow the Recorder of Hades,
\q1 ⌜Belit⌝-seri, and readeth before her; ⌜she lifted⌝ her head (and) beheld me,
\q1 . . . and took this . . . . . . . . . . . . .
\c 8
\cp 8
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.2\vp* “Unto me hearken, O Elders, to me, aye, me ⌜shall ye listen⌝,
\q1 ’Tis that I weep for my ⌜comrade⌝ Enkidu, bitterly crying
\q1 Like to a wailing woman: my grip is ⌜slack’d⌝ on the curtleaxe
\v 2 \vp 1.5\vp* (Slung at) my thigh, (and) the brand at my belt from my sight ⌜is removéd⌝.
\q1 (Aye, and) my festal attire ⌜lends nought of its aid for⌝ my pleasure,
\q1 Me, me hath ⌜sorrow⌝ assailéd, and ⌜cast⌝ me ⌜down in affliction⌝.
\v 3 \vp 1.8\vp* Comrade (and) henchman, who chased the wild ass, the pard of the desert,
\q1 Comrade (and) henchman, who chased the wild ass, the pard of the desert,
\v 4 \vp 1.10\vp* Enkidu—we who all ⌜haps⌝ overcame, ascending ⌜the mountains⌝.
\q1 Captured the Heavenly Bull, and ⌜destroy’d (him)⌝: we o’erthrew Humbaba,
\q1 He who ⌜abode⌝ in the Forest ⌜of Cedars⌝—O, what is this slumber
\q1 Now hath o’ercome ⌜thee⌝, (for now) art thou dark, nor art able to hear ⌜me⌝?”
\v 5 \vp 1.15\vp* Natheless he raised not ⌜his eyes, and⌝ his heart, (when Gilgamish) felt (it),
\q1 Made no beat.
\q1 Then he veil’d (his) friend like a bride ..
\q1 Lifted his voice like a lion . . . . . . . .
\q1 ⌜Roar’d⌝ like a lioness robb’d of ⌜her⌝ whelps. In front of his ⌜comrade⌝
\v 6 \vp 1.20\vp* Paced he backwards and forwards, tearing and casting his ringlets(?),
\q1 Plucking and casting away (all) the grace of his . . . .
\v 7 \vp 1.22\vp* Then when something of morning had dawn’d, did Gilgamish . . . .
\v 8 \vp 2.49-50\vp* “⌜O, on a couch of great size will I, thy friend and thy brother,
\v 9 \vp 3.1\vp* ⌜Gilgamish, grant thee to lie⌝, on ⌜a handsome⌝ couch ⌜will I grant thee
\q1 Rest, and⌝ to sit on ⌜a throne of great size, a throne at (my) left hand⌝,
\q1 So that the princes of Hades ⌜may kiss thy feet (in their homage)⌝;
\q1 I, too, will make (all) ⌜the people of Erech⌝ lament in thy (honour),
\v 10 \vp 3.5\vp* ⌜Making them mourn thee⌝, (and) damsels (and) heroes ⌜constrain to thy service⌝,
\q1 While I myself for thy sake ⌜will cause my body to carry⌝
\q1 ⌜Stains⌝, (and) will put on the skin of a ⌜lion, and range o’er the desert⌝.”
\v 11 \vp 3.8\vp* Then when something of morning had dawn’d did ⌜Gilgamish⌝ . . . .
\q1 Loosing his girdle . . . . . . . . . . . . . . .
\v 12 \vp 5.43\vp* “ . . . Judge of the Anunnaki . . .”
\q1 (Then), when Gilgamish heard this, he form’d of the slaying a concept.
\v 13 \vp 5.45\vp* (Then), with the dawn of the morning did Gilgamish fashion a . . .
\q1 Brought out also a mighty platter of wood from the highlands.
\q1 Fill’d he with honey a bowl of (bright) ruby, a bowl (too) of azure,
\q1 Fill’d he with cream; (and) adorn’d he the . . ., and Shamash instructed ..
\c 9
\cp 9
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* Gilgamish bitterly wept for his comrade, (for) Enkidu, ranging
\q1 Over the desert: “I, too—shall I not die like Enkidu also?
\v 2 \vp 1.5\vp* Sorrow hath enter’d my heart; I fear death as I range o’er the desert,
\q1 I will get hence on the road to the presence of Uta-Napishtim,
\q1 —Offspring of Ubara-Tutu is he—and with speed will I travel.
\q1 (If) ’tis in darkness that I shall arrive at the Gates of the Mountains,
\v 3 \vp 1.10\vp* Meeting with lions, then terror fall on me, I’ll lift my head (skywards),
\q1 Offer my prayer to the Moon-god, (or else) to . . the gods let my orison
\q1 Come . . . ‘O deliver me!’” . . . He slept . . . (and) a dream . . .
\q1 ⌜Saw he⌝ . . . which were rejoicing in life,
\v 4 \vp 1.15\vp* Poised he ⌜his⌝ axe . . . in his hand, (and) drew ⌜his glaive from⌝ his baldric,
\q1 Lance-like leapt he amongst them . . . smiting, . . . (and) crushing.
\v 5 \vp 2.1\vp* Mashu the name of the hills; as he reach’d the Mountains of Mashu,
\q1 Where ev’ry day they keep watch o’er ⌜the Sun-god’s⌝ rising ⌜and setting⌝,
\v 6 \vp 2.5\vp* Unto the Zenith of Heaven ⌜uprear’d are⌝ their summits, (and) downwards
\q1 (Deep) unto Hell reach their breasts: (and there) at their portals stand sentry
\q1 Scorpion-men, awful in terror, their (very) glance Death: (and) tremendous,
\q1 Shaking the hills, their magnificence; they are the Wardens of Shamash,
\v 7 \vp 2.10\vp* Both at his rising and setting. (No sooner) did Gilgamish see them”
\q1 (Than) from alarm and dismay was his countenance stricken with pallor,
\q1 Senseless, he grovell’d before them.
\q1 (Then) unto his wife spake the Scorpion:
\q1 “Lo, he that cometh to us—’tis the flesh of the gods is his body.”
\v 8 \vp 2.15\vp* (Then) to the Scorpion-man answer⌝d his wife: “Two parts of him god-(like),
\q1 (Only) a third of him human.”
\v 9 \vp 5.48\vp* Bearing its fruit (all) ruby, and hung about with (its) tendrils.
\v 10 \vp 5.50\vp* Fair for beholding, and azure the boskage it bore; (aye), ’twas bearing
\q1 Fruits (all) desirable unto the eye.
\v 11 \vp 6b.2\vp* “⌜He of the wild things hath dresséd⌝ their pelts and the flesh of them eateth.
\q1 Gilgamish, ⌜never⌝ a crossing ⌜shall be (?)⌝ where none hath been ever,
\q1 (No), ⌜so long⌝ as the gale driveth water.”
\v 12 \vp 6b.5\vp* Shamash was touch’d, that he summon’d him, (thus) unto Gilgamish speaking:
\q1 “Gilgamish, why dost thou run, (forasmuch as) the life which thou seekest
\q1 Thou shalt not find?” (Whereat) Gilgamish answer’d the warrior Shamash:
\v 13 \vp 6b.10\vp* “Shall I, after I roam up and down o’er the waste as a wand’rer,
\q1 Lay my head in the bowels of earth, and throughout the years slumber
\q1 Ever and aye? Let mine eyes see the Sun and be sated with brightness,
\q1 (Yea, for) the darkness is (banish’d) afar, if wide be the brightness.
\q1 When will the man who is dead (ever) look on the light of the Sunshine?”
\c 10
\cp 10
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* Dwelt Siduri, the maker of wine . . . .
\q1 Wine(?) was her trade, her trade was . . . . . . . . .
\q1 Cover’d she was with a veil and . . . . . . . . .
\v 2 \vp 1.5\vp* Gilgamish wander’d ⌜towards her⌝ . . . . . . . . .
\q1 Pelts was he wearing . . . . . . . . .
\q1 Flesh of the gods in ⌜his body⌝ possessing, but woe in ⌜his belly⌝,
\q1 (Aye), and his countenance like to a (man) who hath gone a far journey.
\v 3 \vp 1.10\vp* Look’d in the distance the maker of wine, (and) a word in her bosom
\q1 Quoth she, in thought with herself: “This is one who would ravish (?) ⌜a woman⌝,
\v 4 \vp 1.15\vp* Whither doth he advance in . . . ?” As soon as the Wine-maker saw him,
\q1 Barr’d she ⌜her postern⌝, barr’d she her inner door, barr’d she ⌜her chamber(?)⌝.
\q1 Straightway did Gilgamish, too, in his turn catch the sound ⌜of her shutting(?)⌝,
\q1 Lifted his chin, and so did he let ⌜his attention fall on her⌝.
\v 5 \vp 1.19\vp* Unto her (therefore) did Gilgamish ⌜speak, to the Wine-maker saying⌝:
\v 6 \vp 1.20\vp* “Wine-maker, what didst thou see, that ⌜thy postern (now)⌝ thou hast barréd,
\q1 Barréd thine inner door, ⌜barréd thy chamber(?)⌝? O, I’ll smite ⌜thy⌝ portal,
\q1 ⌜Breaking the bolt⌝ . . . . . . . . . . .
\v 7 \vp 1.23\vp* ⌜Unto him (answer’d) the Wine-maker, speaking to Gilgamish, (saying):
\q1 “Why is thy vigour (so) wasted, (or why) is thy countenance sunken,
\q1 (Why) hath thy spirit a sorrow (?), (or why) hath thy cheerfulness surcease?
\v 8 \vp 1.35\vp* (O, but) there’s woe in thy belly! Like one who hath gone a far journey
\q1 (So) is thy face—(O,) with cold and with heat is thy countenance weather’d,
\q1 . . . that thou shouldst range over the desert.”
\q1 Gilgamish unto her (answer’d and) spake to the Wine-maker, saying:
\v 9 \vp 1.40\vp* “Wine-maker, ’tis not my vigour is wasted, nor countenance sunken,
\q1 Nor hath my spirit a sorrow (?), (forsooth), nor my cheerfulness surcease,
\q1 No, ’tis not woe in my belly: nor doth my visage resemble
\q1 One who hath gone a far journey—nor is my countenance weather’d
\v 10 \vp 1.45\vp* Either by cold or by heat . . . that (thus) I range over the desert.
\q1 Comrade (and) henchman, who chased the wild ass, the pard of the desert,
\q1 Comrade (and) henchman, who chased the wild ass, the pard of the desert,
\q1 Enkidu—we who all haps overcame, ascending the mountains,
\v 11 \vp 1.50\vp* Captured the Heavenly Bull, and destroy’d him⌝: we ⌜o’erthrew Humbaba,
\q1 He who abode in the Forest of Cedars; we slaughter’d the lions
\v 12 \vp 2.1\vp* There in the Gates (?) of the mountains (?); with me enduring all hardships,
\q1 Enkidu, (he was) my comrade—the lions we slaughter’d (together),
\q1 (Aye), enduring all hardships—and him his fate hath o’ertaken.
\q1 (So) did I mourn him six days, (yea), a se’nnight, until unto burial
\q1 I could consign (?) him . . . . (then) did I fear . . . . .
\q1 Death did I dread, that I range o’er the desert⌝: the hap of my comrade
\q1 ⌜Lay on me heavy(?)—O ’tis a long road that I range o’er⌝ the desert!
\q1 Enkidu, (yea), ⌜of my comrade the hap lay heavy (?) upon me⌝—
\v 13 \vp 2.10\vp* ⌜’Tis a long road⌝ that I range ⌜o’er the desert—O, how to be silent⌝,
\q1 (Aye, or) how to give voice? ⌜(For) the comrade I ha’ (so) lovéd⌝
\q1 Like to the dust ⌜hath become⌝; O Enkidu, (he was) my comrade,
\q1 He whom I loved hath become ⌜like the dust⌝—⌜I,⌝ shall I not, also,
\q1 Lay me down ⌜like him⌝, throughout all eternity ⌜never returning⌝?”
\v 14 \vp 2b.1\vp* “He who enduréd all hardships with me, whom I lovéd dearly,
\q1 Enkidu,—he who enduréd all hardships with me (is now perish’d),
\q1 Gone to the common lot of mankind! (And) I have bewail’d him
\v 15 \vp 2b.5\vp* Day and night long: (and) unto the tomb I have not consign’d him.
\q1 (O but) my friend cometh not (?) to my call—six days, (yea), a se’nnight
\v 16 \vp 2b.10\vp* He like a worm hath lain on his face—(and) I for this reason
\q1 Find no life, (but must needs) roam the desert like to a hunter,
\q1 (Wherefore), O Wine-maker, now that (at last) I look on thy visage,
\q1 Death which I dread I will see not!”
\v 17 \vp 2b.14\vp* The Wine-maker Gilgamish answer’d:
\v 18 \vp 3.1\vp* “Gilgamish, why runnest thou, (inasmuch as) the life which thou seekest,
\q1 Thou canst not find? (For) the gods, in their (first) creation of mortals,
\v 19 \vp 3.5\vp* Death allotted to man, (but) life they retain’d in their keeping.
\q1 Gilgamish, full be thy belly,
\q1 Each day and night be thou merry, (and) daily keep holiday revel,
\v 20 \vp 3.10\vp* Each day and night do thou dance and rejoice; (and) fresh be thy raiment,
\q1 (Aye), let thy head be clean washen, (and) bathe thyself in the water,
\q1 Cherish the little one holding thy hand; be thy spouse in thy bosom
\q1 Happy—(for) this is the dower ⌜of man⌝ . . . . .
\v 21 \vp 3.15\vp* ⌜Gilgamish⌝ (thus) continued his speech to the Wine-maker, (saying),
\q1 “⌜Pr’ythee, then⌝, Wine-maker, which is the way unto Uta-Napishtim?
\q1 ⌜What (is)⌝ its token, I pr’ythee, vouchsafe me, vouchsafe me its token.
\q1 If it be possible (even) the Ocean (itself) will I traverse,
\q1 (But) if it should be impossible, (then) will I range o’er the desert.”
\v 22 \vp 3.20\vp* (Thus) did the Wine-maker answer to him, unto Gilgamish (saying),
\q1 “There hath been never a crossing, O Gilgamish: never aforetime
\q1 Anyone, coming thus far, hath been able to traverse the Ocean:
\q1 Warrior Shamash doth cross it, ’tis true, but who besides Shamash
\q1 Maketh the traverse? (Yea), rough is the ferry, (and) rougher its passage,
\v 23 \vp 3.25\vp* (Aye), too, ’tis deep are the Waters of Death, which bar its approaches.
\q1 Gilgamish, if perchance thou succeed in traversing the Ocean,
\q1 What wilt thou do, when unto the Waters of Death thou arrivest?
\q1 Gilgamish, there is Ur-Shanabi, boatman to Uta-Napishtim,
\q1 He with whom sails (?) are, the urnu of which in the forest he plucketh,
\v 24 \vp 3.30\vp* (Now) let him look on thy presence, (and) ⌜if it be⌝ possible with him
\q1 Cross—(but) if it be not, (then) do thou retrace thy steps (homewards).”
\v 25 \vp 3.32\vp* Gilgamish, hearing this, ⌜taketh⌝ (his) axe in his ⌜hand⌝, ⌜while he draweth Glaive from his baldric (?)⌝.
\v 26 \vp 3b.2\vp* (Then) did Ur-Shanabi speak to him (yea), unto Gilgamish, (saying):
\q1 “Tell to me what is thy name, (for) I am Ur-Shanabi, (henchman),
\q1 (Aye), of far Uta-Napishtim.” To him did Gilgamish answer:
\v 27 \vp 3b.5\vp* “Gilgamish, (that) is my name, come hither from Erech(?), E-Anni (?),
\q1 (One) who hath traversed the Mountains, a wearisome journey of Sunrise,
\v 28 \vp 3b.10\vp* Now that I look on thy face, Ur-Shanabi—Uta-Napishtim
\q1 Let me see also—the Distant one!” Him did Ur-Shanabi ⌜answer⌝,
\q1 Gilgamish: . . . . . . . . .”
\v 29 \vp 3c.30\vp* (Thus) did Ur-Shanabi speak to him, (yea), unto Gilgamish, (saying):
\q1 “Why is thy vigour all wasted . . .”
\v 30 \vp 3c.32\vp* Gilgamish (thus) continued his speech to Ur-Shanabi, (saying):
\q1 “Pr’ythee, Ur-Shanabi, which is ⌜the way unto Uta-Napishtim⌝?
\q1 What is its token, I pr’ythee, vouchsafe me, vouchsafe me ⌜its token⌝.
\q1 If it be possible (even) the Ocean (itself) will I traverse,
\v 31 \vp 3c.35\vp* But if it should be impossible, ⌜(then) will I range o’er the desert⌝.”
\v 32 \vp 3c.36\vp* (Thus) did Ur-Shanabi speak to him, (yea), unto Gilgamish, (saying):
\q1 “Gilgamish, ’tis thine own hand hath hinder’d ⌜thy crossing the Ocean⌝,
\q1 Thou hast destroyéd the sails(?), (and) hast piercéd (?) the . . .
\q1 (Now) destroy’d are the sails(?), and the urnu not . . . . .
\v 33 \vp 3c.40\vp* Gilgamish, take thee thy axe in ⌜thy⌝ hand; O, descend to the forest,
\q1 ⌜Fashion thee⌝ poles each of five gar in length; make (knops of) bitumen,
\q1 Sockets, (too), add (to them): bring ⌜them me⌝.” (Thereat), when Gilgamish ⌜heard this⌝,
\q1 Took he the axe in his hand, (and) ⌜the glaive⌝ drew forth ⌜from his baldric⌝,
\v 34 \vp 3c.45\vp* Went to the forest, and poles each of five gar in length ⌜did he fashion⌝,
\q1 (Knops of) bitumen he made, and he added (their) sockets: and brought them . .,
\q1 Gilgamish (then), and Ur-Shanabi fared them forth ⌜in their vessel⌝,
\q1 Launch’d they the boat on the billow, and they themselves ⌜in her embarking⌝.
\q1 After the course of a month and a half he saw on the third day
\v 35 \vp 3c.50\vp* How that Ur-Shanabi (now) at the Waters of Death had arrivéd.
\v 36 \vp 4.1\vp* (Thus) did Ur-Shanabi ⌜answer⌝ him, ⌜(yea), unto Gilgamish, (saying)⌝:
\q1 “Gilgamish, take the . . . . away . . . . . . . . . . .
\q1 Let not the Waters of Death touch thy hand . . . . . .
\q1 Gilgamish, take thou a second, a third, and a fourth pole (for thrusting),
\v 37 \vp 4.5\vp* Gilgamish, take thou a fifth, (and) a sixth, and a seventh (for thrusting),
\q1 Gilgamish, take thou an eighth, (and) a ninth, and a tenth pole (for thrusting),
\q1 Gilgamish, take an eleventh, a twelfth pole!” He ceased from (his) poling,
\q1 (Aye) with twice-sixty (thrusts); (then) ungirded his loins . . . .
\v 38 \vp 4.10\vp* Gilgamish . . . . (?), and set up the mast in its socket.
\v 39 \vp 4.11\vp* Uta-Napishtim look’d into the distance and, inwardly musing,
\v 40 \vp 4.15\vp* Said to himself: “(Now), why are ⌜the sails(?)⌝ of the vessel destroyéd,
\q1 Aye, and one who is not of my . . . (?) doth ride on the vessel?
\q1 (This) is no mortal who cometh: nor . . . .
\q1 I look, but (this) is no ⌜mortal⌝ . . . . . .
\v 41 \vp 4.20\vp* I look, but . . . . . I look but . . . . .
\v 42 \vp 4.23\vp* Gilgamish (thus) continued his speech unto Uta-Napishtim,
\q1 “Then ⌜I bethought me⌝, I’ll get hence and see what far Uta-Napishtim
\v 43 \vp 4.25\vp* Saith (on the matter) . (And so), again (?) I came through all countries,
\q1 Travell’d o’er difficult mountains, (aye), ⌜and⌝ all seas have I traversed,
\q1 Nor hath (ever) my face had its fill of gentle sleep (?): (but) with hardship
\q1 Have I exhausted myself, (and) my flesh have I laden with sorrow.
\v 44 \vp 4.30\vp* Ere I had come to the ⌜House(?)⌝ of the Wine-Maker, spent were my garments,
\q1 . . . Owl, bat, lion, pard, wild cat, deer, ibex, and . . . . . .
\q1 ⌜Flesh⌝ of them (all) have I eaten, (and eke) their pelts have I dress’d (?) ⌜me⌝.”
\v 45 \vp 6.26\vp* “Shall we for ever build house(s), for ever set signet (to contract),
\q1 Brothers continue to share, or among ⌜foes (?)⌝ always be hatred?
\q1 (Or) will for ever the stream (that hath risen) in spate bring a torrent,
\q1 Kulilu-bird ⌜to⌝ Kirippu-bird . . . . . . . . . ?
\q1 Face which doth look on the sunlight . . . presently (?) shall not be . . .
\q1 Sleeping and dead ⌜are⌝ alike, from Death they mark no distinction
\q1 Servant and master, when once thy have reach’d ⌜their full span allotted⌝,
\q1 Then do the Anunnaki, great gods, . . . . . .
\q1 Mammetum, Maker of Destiny with them, doth destiny settle,
\q1 Death, (aye), and Life they determine; of Death is the day not revealéd.”
\c 11
\cp 11
\p
\v 1 \vp 1.1\vp* Gilgamish unto him spake, to Uta-Napishtim the Distant:
\q1 “Uta-Napishtim, upon thee I gaze, (yet) in no wise thy presence
\q1 Strange is, (for) thou art like me, and in no wise different art thou;
\v 2 \vp 1.5\vp* Thou art like me; (yea) a stomach for fighting doth make thee consummate,
\q1 ⌜Aye, and to rest (?)⌝ on thy back thou dost lie. ⌜O tell me (?)⌝, how couldst thou
\q1 Stand in th’ Assemblage of Gods to petition for life (everlasting)?”
\v 3 \vp 1.8\vp* Uta-Napishtim (addressing him thus) unto Gilgamish answer’d:
\q1 “Gilgamish, I unto thee will discover the (whole) hidden story,
\v 4 \vp 1.10\vp* Aye, and the rede of the Gods will I tell thee.
\q1 The City Shurippak—
\q1 (O ’tis) a city thou knowest!—is set ⌜on the marge⌝ of Euphrates,
\q1 Old is this city, with gods in its midst. (Now), the great gods a deluge
\q1 Purposed to bring: . . . . . . there was Anu, their sire; their adviser
\q1 Warrior Enlil; Ninurta, their herald; their leader(?) Ennugi;
\q1 Nin-igi-azag—’tis Ea—, (albeit) conspirator with them,
\v 5 \vp 1.20\vp* Unto a reed-hut their counsel betray’d he: “O Reed-hut, O Reed-hut!
\q1 Wall, wall! Hearken, O Reed-hut, consider, O Wall! O thou Mortal,
\q1 Thou of Shurippak, thou scion of Ubara-Tutu, a dwelling
\v 6 \vp 1.25\vp* Pull down, (and) fashion a vessel (therewith); abandon possessions,
\q1 Life do thou seek, (and) thy hoard disregard, and save life; every creature
\q1 Make to embark in the vessel. The vessel, which thou art to fashion,
\v 7 \vp 1.30\vp* Apt be its measure; its beam and its length be in due correspondence,
\q1 (Then) ⌜on⌝ the deep do thou launch it.” And I—sooth, I apprehending,
\q1 (This wise) to Ea, my lord, did I speak: ‘⌜See⌝, Lord, what thou sayest
\v 8 \vp 1.35\vp* Thus, do I honour, I’ll do—(but) to city, to people, and elders
\q1 Am I, forsooth, to explain?’ (Then) Ea made answer in speaking,
\q1 Saying to me—me, his henchman!—‘Thou mortal, shalt speak to them this wise:
\q1 “’Tis me alone (?) whom Enlil so hateth that I in your city
\v 9 \vp 1.40\vp* No (more) may dwell, nor turn my face unto the land which is Enlil’s.
\q1 ⌜I will go⌝ down to the Deep, (there) dwelling with Ea, my ⌜liege⌝ lord,
\q1 (Wherefore) ⌜on⌝ you will he shower down plenty, yea, fowl ⌜in great number(?)⌝,
\v 10 \vp 1.45\vp* Booty of fish . . . . ⌜and big⌝ the harvest.
\q1 . . . . . . . . causing a plentiful rainfall (?) to come down upon you.”’
\v 11 \vp 1.47\vp* ⌜(Then) when something⌝ of morning had dawn’d . . . .
\v 12 \vp 1.55\vp* Pitch did the children provide, (while) the strong brought ⌜all⌝ that was needful.
\q1 (Then) on the fifth day (after) I laid out the shape (of my vessel),
\q1 Ten gar each was the height of her sides, in accord with her planning(?),
\q1 Ten gar to match was the size of her deck (?), and the shape of the forepart (?)
\v 13 \vp 1.60\vp* Did I lay down, (and) the same did I fashion; (aye), six times cross-pinn’d her,
\q1 Sevenfold did I divide her . . . ., divided her inwards
\q1 Ninefold: hammer’d the caulking within her, (and) found me a quant-pole,
\v 14 \vp 1.65\vp* (All) that was needful I added; the hull with six shar of bitumen
\q1 Smear’d I, (and) three shar of pitch ⌜did I smear⌝ on the inside; some people,
\q1 Bearing a vessel of grease, three shar of it brought (me); (and) one shar
\q1 (Out of this) grease did I leave, which the tackling (?) consumed; (and) the boatman
\v 15 \vp 1.70\vp* Two shar of grease stow’d away; (yea), beeves for the . . . I slaughter’d,
\q1 Each day lambs did I slay: mead, beer, oil, wine, too, the workmen
\q1 ⌜Drank⌝ as though they were water, and made a great feast like the New Year,
\v 16 \vp 1.80\vp* ⌜All I possess’d I⌝ laded aboard her; the silver I laded
\q1 All I possess’d; gold, all I possess’d I laded aboard her,
\q1 All I possess’d of the seed of all living ⌜I laded aboard⌝ her.
\q1 Into the ship I embark’d all my kindred and family (with me),
\v 17 \vp 1.85\vp* Cattle (and) beasts of the field (and) all handicraftsmen embarking.
\q1 (Then) decreed Shamash the hour: “ . . . . (?)
\q1 Shall in the night let a plentiful rainfall(?) pour down . . . .
\q1 (Then) do thou enter the vessel, and (straightway) shut down thy hatchway.”
\v 18 \vp 1.90\vp* Came (then) that hour (appointed), . . . . . . (?)
\q1 Did in the night let a plentiful rainfall(?) pour down . . . . (?)
\q1 View’d I the aspect of day: to look on the day bore a horror,
\q1 (Wherefore) I enter’d the vessel, and (straightway) shut down my hatchway,
\q1 (So, too) to shut down the vessel to Puzur-Amurri (?), the boatman,
\v 19 \vp 1.95\vp* Did I deliver the poop (of the ship), besides its equipment.
\v 20 \vp 1.96\vp* (Then), when something of dawn had appear’d, from out the horizon
\q1 Rose a cloud darkling; (lo), Adad (the storm-god) was rumbling within it,
\v 21 \vp 1.100\vp* Nabu and Sharru were leading the vanguard, and coming as heralds
\q1 Over the hills and the levels: (then) Irragal wrench’d out the bollards;
\q1 Havoc Ninurta let loose as he came, th’ Anunnaki their torches
\v 22 \vp 1.105\vp* Brandish’d, and shrivell’d the land with their flames; desolation from Adad
\q1 Stretch’d to (high) Heaven, (and) all that was bright was turn’d into darkness.
\v 23 \vp 1.107\vp* Nor could a brother distinguish his brother,; from heaven were mortals
\q1 Not to be spied. O, were stricken with terror the gods at the Deluge,
\q1 Fleeing, they rose to the Heaven of Anu, and crouch’d in the outskirts,
\v 24 \vp 1.115\vp* Cow’ring like curs were the gods (while) like to a woman in travail
\q1 Ishtar did cry, she shrieking aloud, (e’en) the sweet-spoken Lady
\q1 (She of the gods): ‘May that day turn to dust, because I spake evil
\v 25 \vp 1.120\vp* (There) in th’ Assemblage of Gods! O, how could I utter (such) evil
\q1 (There) in the Assemblage of Gods, (so) to blot out my people, ordaining
\q1 Havoc! Sooth, then, am I to give birth, unto (these) mine own people
\q1 Only to glut (with their bodies) the Sea as though they were fish-spawn?’
\v 26 \vp 1.125\vp* Gods—Anunnaki—wept with her, the gods were sitting (all) humbled,
\q1 (Aye), in (their) weeping, (and) closed were their lips ⌜amid(?)⌝ the Assemblage.
\q1 Six days, a se’nnight the hurricane, deluge, (and) tempest continued
\q1 Sweeping the land: when the seventh day came, were quelléd the warfare,
\v 27 \vp 1.130\vp* Tempest (and) deluge which like to an army embattail’d were fighting.
\q1 Lull’d was the sea, (all) spent was the gale, assuaged was the deluge,
\q1 (So) did I look on the day; (lo), sound was (all) still’d; and all human
\q1 Back to (its) clay was return’d, and fen was level with roof-tree.
\v 28 \vp 1.135\vp* (Then) I open’d a hatchway, and down on my cheek stream’d the sunlight,
\q1 Bowing myself, I sat weeping, my tears o’er my cheek(s) overflowing,
\q1 Into the distance I gazed, to the furthest bounds of the Ocean,
\v 29 \vp 1.140\vp* Land was uprear’d at twelve (points), and the Ark on the Mountain of Nisir
\q1 Grounded; the Mountain of Nisir held fast, nor gave lease to her shifting.
\q1 One day, (nay,) two, did Nisir hold fast, nor give lease to her shifting.
\q1 Three days, (nay), four, did Nisir hold fast, nor give lease to her shifting,
\q1 Five days, (nay,) six, did Nisir hold fast, nor give lease to her shifting.
\v 30 \vp 1.145\vp* (Then), when the seventh day dawn’d, I put forth a dove, and released (her),
\q1 (But) to and fro went the dove, and return’d (for) a resting-place was not.
\v 31 \vp 1.150\vp* (Then) I a swallow put forth and released; to and fro went the swallow,
\q1 She (too) return’d, (for) a resting-place was not; I put forth a raven,
\q1 Her, (too,) releasing; the raven went, too, and th’ abating of waters
\q1 Saw; and she ate as she waded (and) splash’d, (unto me) not returning.
\v 32 \vp 1.155\vp* Unto the four winds (of heaven) I freed (all the beasts), and an off’ring
\q1 Sacrificed, and a libation I pour’d on the peak of the mountain,
\q1 Twice seven flagons devoting, (and) sweet cane, (and) cedar, and myrtle,
\v 33 \vp 1.160\vp* Heap’d up beneath them; the gods smelt the savour, the gods the sweet savour
\q1 Smelt; (aye,) the gods did assemble like flies o’er him making the off’ring.
\q1 Then, on arriving, the Queen (of the gods) the magnificent jewels
\q1 Lifted on high, which Anu had made in accord with her wishes;
\q1 ‘O ye Gods! I will (rather) forget (this) my necklet of sapphires,
\v 34 \vp 1.165\vp* Than not maintain these days in remembrance, nor ever forget them.
\q1 (So), though (the rest of) the gods may present themselves at the off’ring,
\q1 Enlil (alone of the gods) may (himself) not come to the off’ring,
\q1 Because he, unreasoning, brought on a deluge, and therefore my people
\q1 Unto destruction consign’d.’
\v 35 \vp 1.170\vp* Then Enlil, on his arrival,
\q1 Spied out the vessel, and (straightway) did Enlil burst into anger,
\q1 Swollen with wrath ’gainst the gods, the Igigi: ‘Hath any of mortals
\q1 ’Scaped? Sooth, never a man could have lived through (the welter of) ruin.’
\q1 (Then) did Ninurta make answer and speak unto warrior Enlil,
\v 36 \vp 1.175\vp* Saying: ‘O, who can there be to devise such a plan, except Ea?
\q1 Surely, ’tis Ea is privy to ev’ry design.’ Whereat Ea
\q1 Answer’d and spake unto Enlil, the warrior, saying: ‘O chieftain
\q1 Thou of the gods, thou warrior! How, forsooth, how (all) uncounsell’d
\v 37 \vp 1.180\vp* Couldst thou a deluge bring on? (Aye,) visit his sin on the sinner
\q1 Visit his guilt on the guilty, (but) O, have mercy, that (thereby)
\q1 He shall not be cut off; be clement, that he may not ⌜perish⌝.
\q1 O, instead of thy making a flood, let a lion come, man to diminish;
\q1 O, instead of thy making a flood, let a jackal come, man to diminish;
\q1 O, instead of thy making a flood, let a famine occur, that the country
\v 38 \vp 1.185\vp* May be ⌜devour’d(?)⌝; instead of thy making a flood, let the Plague-god
\q1 Come and the people ⌜o’erwhelm⌝;
\q1 Sooth, indeed ’twas not I of the Great Gods the secret revealéd,
\q1 (But) to th’ Abounding in Wisdom vouchsafed I a dream, and (in this wise)
\q1 He of the gods heard the secret. Deliberate, now, on his counsel’.
\v 39 \vp 1.190\vp* (Then) to the Ark came up Enlil; my hand did he grasp, and uplifted
\q1 Me, even me, and my wife, too, he raised, and, bent-kneed beside me,
\q1 Made her to kneel; our foreheads he touch’d as he stood there between us,
\q1 Blessing us; ‘Uta-Napishtim hath hitherto only been mortal,
\q1 Now, indeed, Uta-Napishtim and (also) his wife shall be equal
\v 40 \vp 1.195\vp* Like to us gods; in the distance afar at the mouth of the rivers
\q1 Uta-Napishtim shall dwell’. (So) they took me and (there) in the distance
\q1 Caused me to dwell at the mouth of the rivers.
\q1 But thee, as for thee, pray,
\q1 Who will assemble the gods for thy (need), that the life which thou seekest
\q1 Thou mayst discover? Come, fall not asleep for six days, aye, a se’nnight!”
\v 41 \vp 1.200\vp* (Then), while he sat on his haunches a sleep like a breeze breathed upon him.
\q1 Spake to her, Uta-Napishtim, yea, unto his wife: “O, behold him,
\q1 E’en the strong fellow who asketh for life, (how) hath breathéd upon him
\v 42 \vp 1.205\vp* Sleep like a breeze!” (Then) his wife unto Uta-Napishtim the Distant
\q1 Answer’d: “O, touch him, and let the man wake, that the road he hath traversed
\q1 He may betake himself homeward in peace, that he by the portal
\q1 Whence he fared forth may return to his land.” Spake Uta-Napishtim,
\v 43 \vp 1.210\vp* (Yea), to his wife: “(How) the troubles of mortals do trouble thee also!
\q1 Bake then his flour (and) put at his head, but the time he is sleeping
\q1 On the house-wall do thou mark it.” (So straightway) she (did so), his flour
\q1 Baked she (and) set at his head, but the time he was sleeping she noted
\v 44 \vp 1.215\vp* On the house-wall. (So), first was collected his flour, (then) secondly sifted,
\q1 Thirdly, ’twas moisten’d, and fourthly she kneaded his dough, and so fifthly
\q1 Leaven she added, and sixthly ’twas baked; (then) seventh—he touch’d him,
\q1 All on a sudden, and (so from his slumber) awoke the (great) fellow!
\v 45 \vp 1.219\vp* Gilgamish unto him spake, (yea) to Uta-Napishtim the Distant:
\v 46 \vp 1.220\vp* “(Tell me), I pr’ythee (?), was ’t thou, who when sleep was shower’d upon me
\q1 All on a sudden didst touch me, and (straightway) rouse me (from slumber)?”
\q1 Uta-Napishtim to Gilgamish ⌜spake, (yea), unto him spake he⌝:
\q1 “Gilgamish, told was the tale of thy meal . . . and (then) did I wake thee:
\v 47 \vp 1.225\vp* ⌜‘One’—was collected⌝ thy flour: ⌜(then) ‘two’⌝—it was sifted; (and) ‘thirdly’—
\q1 Moisten’d: (and) ‘fourthly’—she kneaded thy dough ⌜(and) ‘fifthly’⌝ the leaven
\q1 Added: (and) ‘sixthly’—’twas baked: ⌜(and) ‘seventh’⌝ —’twas I on a sudden
\q1 Touch’d thee and thou didst awake.” To Uta-Napishtim, the Distant,
\v 48 \vp 1.230\vp* Gilgamish answer’d: “O, ⌜how⌝ shall I act, (or) where shall I hie me,
\q1 Uta-Napishtim? A Robber (from me) hath ravish’d my ⌜courage,⌝
\q1 Death ⌜in⌝ my bed-chamber broodeth, and Death is wherever I ⌜listen⌝ .”
\v 49 \vp 1.233\vp* ⌜Spake⌝ to ⌜him, (yea),⌝ to the boatman Ur-Shanabi Uta-Napishtim:
\v 50 \vp 1.235\vp* “’Tis thou, Ur-Shanabi . . . the crossing, will hate thee,
\q1 (Sooth), to all those who come to its marge, doth its marge set a limit:
\q1 (This) man for whom thou wert guide—are stains to cover his body,
\q1 Or shall a skin hide the grace of his limbs? Ur-Shanabi, take him,
\v 51 \vp 1.240\vp* Lead him to where he may bathe, that he wash off his stains in the water
\q1 (White) as the snow: let him cast off his pelt(s) that the sea may remove (them);
\q1 Fair let his body appear: of his head be the fillet renewéd,
\q1 Let him, as clothes for his nakedness, garb himself in a mantle,
\v 52 \vp 1.245\vp* Such that, or ever he come to his city, and finish his journey,
\q1 No (sign of) age shall the mantle betray, but preserve (all) its freshness.”
\q1 Wherefore Ur-Shanabi took him, and where he might bathe did he lead him,
\q1 Washing his stains in the ⌜water⌝ like snow, his pelt(s), ⌜too⌝, discarding,
\v 53 \vp 1.250\vp* So that the sea might bear them away; (and) his body appearéd
\q1 Fair; ⌜of⌝ his head he ⌜the fillet⌝ renewed, and himself in a mantle
\q1 Garb’d, as the clothes for his nakedness, ⌜such that or ever his city
\q1 Reach he⌝, or ever he finish his journey, ⌜the mantle betray not
\v 54 \vp 1.255\vp* Age, but⌝ preserve ⌜(all) its freshness⌝.
\q1 (So) into their vessel embarkéd
\q1 Gilgamish, (aye), and Ur-Shanabi, launching (their) craft ⌜on the billow⌝,
\q1 They themselves riding aboard (her).
\v 55 \vp 1.258\vp* To Uta-Napishtim, the Distant,
\q1 Spake (then) his wife: “Came Gilgamish (hither) aweary with rowing,
\v 56 \vp 1.260\vp* What wilt thou give wherewith he return to his land?” and the meanwhile
\q1 Gilgamish, lifting his pole, was pushing the boat at the seashore.
\q1 (Then answer’d) Uta-Napishtim to him, (yea), ⌜to⌝ Gilgamish ⌜spake he⌝:
\q1 “Gilgamish, (hither) didst come (all) aweary with rowing; (O, tell me),
\v 57 \vp 1.265\vp* What shall I give thee (as gift) wherewith to return to thy country?
\q1 Gilgamish, I will reveal thee a hidden matter . . . I’ll tell thee:
\q1 There is a plant like a thorn with its root (?) ⌜deep down in the ocean⌝,
\q1 Like unto those of the briar (in sooth) its prickles will scratch ⌜thee⌝,
\v 58 \vp 1.270\vp* (Yet) if thy hand reach this plant, ⌜thou’lt surely find life (everlasting)⌝ .”
\q1 (Then), when Gilgamish heard this, he loosen’d(?) ⌜his girdle about him⌝,
\q1 Bound heavy stones ⌜on his feet⌝, which dragg’d him down to the sea-deeps,
\q1 ⌜Found he the plant⌝; as he seized on the plant, (lo), ⌜its prickles did scratch him⌝.
\v 59 \vp 1.275\vp* Cut he the heavy stones ⌜from his feet⌝ that again it restore him
\q1 Unto its shore.
\v 60 \vp 1.277\vp* Gilgamish spake to him, (yea), to the boatman Ur-Shanabi (this wise):
\q1 “(Nay, but) this plant is a plant of great wonder(?), Ur-Shanabi,” said he,
\q1 “Whereby a man may attain his desire—I’ll take it to Erech,
\v 61 \vp 1.280\vp* (Erech), the high-wall’d, and give it to eat ⌜unto . . . .⌝.
\q1 ‘Greybeard-who-turneth-to-man-in-his-prime’ is its name and I’ll eat it
\q1 I myself, that again I may come to my youthful condition.”
\v 62 \vp 1.283\vp* Broke they their fast at the fortieth hour: at the sixtieth rested.
\v 63 \vp 1.285\vp* Gilgamish spied out a pool of cool water, (and) therein descending
\q1 Bathed in the water. (But here was) a serpent who snuff’d the plant’s fragrance,
\q1 Darted he up ⌜from the water (?)⌝, and snatch’d the plant, uttering malison
\v 64 \vp 1.290\vp* As he drew back. Then Gilgamish sate him, (and) burst into weeping.
\q1 Over his cheeks flow’d his tears: to the boatman Ur-Shanabi ⌜spake he(?)⌝
\q1 “(Pr’ythee), ⌜for⌝ whom have toiléd mine arms, O Ur-Shanabi, (tell me),
\v 65 \vp 1.295\vp* (Pr’ythee), for whom hath my heart’s blood been spent? (yea), not for mine own self,
\q1 Have I the guerdon achieved; (no), ’tis for an earth-lion (only)
\q1 Have I the guerdon secured—(and) now at the fortieth hour
\q1 (Such an) one reiveth (it)—O, when I open’d the sluice and . . .ed the attachment,
\q1 (Aye), I noted the sign (?) which to me was vouchsafed as a warning,
\v 66 \vp 1.300\vp* Would I had turn’d and abandon’d the boat at the marge (of the ocean)!”
\q1 Broke they their fast at the fortieth hour: at the sixtieth rested,
\q1 (So in the end) to the middle of Erech, the high-wall’d, arrivéd.
\v 67 \vp 1.303\vp* Gilgamish spake to him, (yea), to the boatman Ur-Shanabi (this wise):
\q1 “Do thou, Ur-Shanabi, go up and walk on the ramparts of Erech,
\q1 Look on its base, and take heed of its bricks, if its bricks be not kiln-burnt,
\v 68 \vp 1.305\vp* (Aye), and its ground-work be not bitumen, e’en seven courses,
\q1 One shar the city, (and) one shar the gardens, and one shar the . . . . (?)
\q1 . . . . the Temple of Ishtar, amass’d I three shar and . . . (?) of Erech.
\c 12
